1
00:01:39,599 --> 00:01:41,798
What are you doing? Get out.

2
00:01:41,799 --> 00:01:43,918
Ain't you pleased to see us, boy?
We got you a pizza.

3
00:01:43,919 --> 00:01:46,478
I ain't got nothing for you. Get out.

4
00:01:46,479 --> 00:01:49,758
- Ventilate him, man. Come on.
- You've been warned, boy.

5
00:01:49,759 --> 00:01:51,998
Plenty. Pop him. Deal with him.

6
00:01:51,999 --> 00:01:54,598
You never said nothing about this.

7
00:01:54,599 --> 00:01:57,145
You think I'm scared of you?

8
00:01:58,279 --> 00:01:59,629
Gimme that.

9
00:02:07,159 --> 00:02:08,829
That's on us, dog.

10
00:02:10,079 --> 00:02:11,559
Move.

11
00:02:36,959 --> 00:02:38,838
The Lord Chancellor
wanted to take your mind

12
00:02:38,839 --> 00:02:41,038
on the abolition of jury trials.

13
00:02:41,039 --> 00:02:42,669
Did he, indeed?

14
00:02:42,839 --> 00:02:46,078
I was under the impression the
royal assent had already been received

15
00:02:46,079 --> 00:02:49,261
for that vile piece of legislation,

16
00:02:49,639 --> 00:02:52,367
the Criminal Justice Act 2003.

17
00:02:52,679 --> 00:02:54,078
Where have you been? On holiday?

18
00:02:54,079 --> 00:02:57,638
The government intends to revisit it
either this year or next year.

19
00:02:57,639 --> 00:03:01,989
With an incredible 339 sections

20
00:03:02,359 --> 00:03:03,989
and 38 schedules,

21
00:03:04,719 --> 00:03:07,918
it's already more complicated
than most people can fathom.

22
00:03:07,919 --> 00:03:09,758
Does he want my mind
on how to simplify it

23
00:03:09,759 --> 00:03:12,123
or to cut it out entirely?

24
00:03:12,359 --> 00:03:13,718
They want to give it more teeth.

25
00:03:13,719 --> 00:03:17,918
They're determined to extend
the section on trials without juries.

26
00:03:17,919 --> 00:03:21,198
You'll not find
a single judge signing up for that.

27
00:03:21,199 --> 00:03:23,438
Precious few were consulted.

28
00:03:23,439 --> 00:03:25,758
Juries are not only
becoming less and less reliable,

29
00:03:25,759 --> 00:03:28,478
they're also getting
harder and harder to empanel.

30
00:03:28,479 --> 00:03:31,518
They're no longer economically viable.
I'm not sure they ever were.

31
00:03:31,519 --> 00:03:33,718
What have economics to do with justice?

32
00:03:33,719 --> 00:03:38,998
The government removes
the right to trial by jury at its peril.

33
00:03:38,999 --> 00:03:41,558
It's merely proposing
to reintroduce a section

34
00:03:41,559 --> 00:03:44,918
that would allow defendants
to elect to be before a judge alone.

35
00:03:44,919 --> 00:03:47,478
To further reduce the remit of juries

36
00:03:47,479 --> 00:03:51,878
is to run the risk of putting too much
power in the hands of the state.

37
00:03:51,879 --> 00:03:56,158
Many cases could be dealt with
more efficiently by magistrates.

38
00:03:56,159 --> 00:04:01,478
Their sentencing powers could be further
increased to, say, two years.

39
00:04:01,479 --> 00:04:04,398
The judges wouldn't stand for it.
They'd strike.

40
00:04:04,399 --> 00:04:06,854
That would be the day, Joe.

41
00:04:08,359 --> 00:04:09,549
Try us.

42
00:04:15,199 --> 00:04:17,836
They're ready for you, Judge.

43
00:04:18,159 --> 00:04:21,838
I think you're gonna
have trouble with these defendants.

44
00:04:21,839 --> 00:04:25,718
- They're boys, aren't they?
- They were sent here from the Bailey

45
00:04:25,719 --> 00:04:31,238
supposedly because of the threat
of intimidation from family and friends.

46
00:04:31,239 --> 00:04:33,438
It hasn't deterred
family and friends, Judge.

47
00:04:33,439 --> 00:04:35,678
You might have to bar them from court.

48
00:04:35,679 --> 00:04:39,078
Well, I'd be reluctant to do so.
It is a public trial.

49
00:04:39,079 --> 00:04:41,678
It has to be seen to be fair and open.

50
00:04:41,679 --> 00:04:47,679
Well, I'll make sure there are
plenty of dock officers around then.

51
00:05:03,039 --> 00:05:07,078
Hey. You in the uniform. You leave
my boy alone or you answer to me.

52
00:05:07,079 --> 00:05:08,638
- Don't you threaten me.
- What?

53
00:05:08,639 --> 00:05:10,158
Talk to her like that again, Popeye,
go on.

54
00:05:10,159 --> 00:05:13,069
Just let the judge deal with it.

55
00:05:24,279 --> 00:05:25,869
All rise.

56
00:05:39,679 --> 00:05:45,758
As I read your name, would each
defendant step forward to be identified.

57
00:05:45,759 --> 00:05:47,309
Jesus Balfe.

58
00:05:47,439 --> 00:05:48,949
It's Jez, blood.

59
00:05:49,559 --> 00:05:51,758
Just answer your name, Mr Balfe.

60
00:05:51,759 --> 00:05:55,798
You are charged on an indictment
containing one count of murder

61
00:05:55,799 --> 00:06:00,678
and the particulars are that, on
the night of the 19th of September last

62
00:06:00,679 --> 00:06:04,029
you went with David Nicolaides

63
00:06:04,879 --> 00:06:06,589
and Sky Rudkin

64
00:06:07,639 --> 00:06:11,094
and shot and killed one Simon Jackson.

65
00:06:11,439 --> 00:06:14,518
Simon Jackson?
Did you say Simon Jackson?

66
00:06:14,519 --> 00:06:16,247
Yes, Simon Jackson.

67
00:06:16,639 --> 00:06:18,367
Never heard of him.

68
00:06:19,799 --> 00:06:22,958
Yes, I'm sure you find
Mr Balfe highly entertaining socially,

69
00:06:22,959 --> 00:06:24,158
but this is not a social gathering.

70
00:06:24,159 --> 00:06:28,678
If he's found guilty, he could go
to prison for a very long time.

71
00:06:28,679 --> 00:06:32,718
So I suggest you let everyone
concentrate on the matter in hand.

72
00:06:32,719 --> 00:06:34,119
Mr Harrison.

73
00:06:34,919 --> 00:06:37,598
Do you plead guilty or not guilty?

74
00:06:37,599 --> 00:06:41,118
Are you a joker?
We didn't kill that chief.

75
00:06:41,119 --> 00:06:42,638
Is that not guilty, Mr Balfe?

76
00:06:42,639 --> 00:06:44,238
That's what it sounded like.

77
00:06:44,239 --> 00:06:46,518
Are you deaf, or what? Can't hear him?

78
00:06:46,519 --> 00:06:48,718
Can't hear, man. Can't hear.

79
00:06:48,719 --> 00:06:49,918
Yes, all right.

80
00:06:49,919 --> 00:06:54,078
Now, as reluctant as I am
to conduct this or any other trial

81
00:06:54,079 --> 00:06:55,838
in the absence of the general public,

82
00:06:55,839 --> 00:06:59,958
if there are inappropriate disturbances,
I will clear the court.

83
00:06:59,959 --> 00:07:03,798
- Now, you make no mistake about that.
- It's justice we want, man.

84
00:07:03,799 --> 00:07:08,163
And perhaps you would
like to be the first to go?

85
00:07:08,399 --> 00:07:12,998
Answer the charge correctly
or risk the wrong plea being entered.

86
00:07:12,999 --> 00:07:15,181
Are you pleading guilty?

87
00:07:16,559 --> 00:07:18,709
No. Not guilty.

88
00:07:19,279 --> 00:07:20,599
Thank you.

89
00:07:20,919 --> 00:07:24,101
David Nicolaides. How do you plead?

90
00:07:24,119 --> 00:07:25,439
Not guilty.

91
00:07:25,519 --> 00:07:27,069
Sky Rudkin.

92
00:07:27,359 --> 00:07:31,158
Mr Rudkin, take your hands
out of your pockets.

93
00:07:31,159 --> 00:07:33,718
Do you plead guilty
or not guilty?

94
00:07:33,719 --> 00:07:35,309
Not guilty, sir.

95
00:07:36,519 --> 00:07:39,338
You can all sit down.

96
00:07:40,399 --> 00:07:44,598
Mr Mason Allen,
are we going to hear from you?

97
00:07:44,599 --> 00:07:49,518
My lord, my client, Mr Balfe,
feels he won't get a fair trial

98
00:07:49,519 --> 00:07:51,792
because he's a black man.

99
00:07:52,239 --> 00:07:56,238
That's not an unfamiliar argument.
Would he like a black judge?

100
00:07:56,239 --> 00:08:01,078
He'd prefer a jury made up
of a representative racial mix.

101
00:08:01,079 --> 00:08:04,438
The jury has yet to be sworn,
how does he know it won't be?

102
00:08:04,439 --> 00:08:08,478
He felt it would have been had the trial
remained at the Old Bailey, not here.

103
00:08:08,479 --> 00:08:09,829
Exactly.

104
00:08:10,879 --> 00:08:12,549
Well, we are here.

105
00:08:12,999 --> 00:08:14,838
The jury system isn't perfect,

106
00:08:14,839 --> 00:08:17,038
especially since
the peremptory challenge has gone,

107
00:08:17,039 --> 00:08:18,598
but it's the best we've got.

108
00:08:18,599 --> 00:08:22,598
- It ain't fair, dread.
- I will try to ensure that it is,

109
00:08:22,599 --> 00:08:27,918
that an intelligent, alert and,
above all, fair jury is empanelled.

110
00:08:27,919 --> 00:08:29,283
Come off of it.

111
00:08:29,319 --> 00:08:32,838
Justice isn't about the way
you look or the colour of your skin.

112
00:08:32,839 --> 00:08:34,469
Justice is blind.

113
00:08:35,439 --> 00:08:38,878
Jurors are not there
for the benefit of the defendants.

114
00:08:38,879 --> 00:08:41,318
Juries are there for the greater good

115
00:08:41,319 --> 00:08:45,410
because they are
concerned members of society.

116
00:08:46,159 --> 00:08:47,838
Now, are there any further challenges?

117
00:08:47,839 --> 00:08:51,318
Yes, my lord. There is a small matter
that's troubling the defence.

118
00:08:51,319 --> 00:08:55,318
The prosecution is seeking to hide
the identity of its principal witness

119
00:08:55,319 --> 00:08:58,718
by asking that
she give evidence via a video link.

120
00:08:58,719 --> 00:09:01,078
Now, this is a denial
of a fundamental right

121
00:09:01,079 --> 00:09:04,358
of my client and his co-defendants
to a fair trial.

122
00:09:04,359 --> 00:09:08,038
Live link-up is not unfamiliar,
Mr Michaels.

123
00:09:08,039 --> 00:09:12,598
Under Section 51 (4 ) (a)
of the Criminal Justice Act 2003,

124
00:09:12,599 --> 00:09:15,838
a witness may so appear
provided the court is satisfied

125
00:09:15,839 --> 00:09:21,238
that it's in the interest of
the effective administration of justice.

126
00:09:21,239 --> 00:09:22,958
I'll hear your argument.

127
00:09:22,959 --> 00:09:26,918
Thank you, my lord. Well, we would argue
that such a disembodied device

128
00:09:26,919 --> 00:09:28,518
totally prejudices a jury,

129
00:09:28,519 --> 00:09:32,558
making them believe that
the defendants are so menacing

130
00:09:32,559 --> 00:09:34,678
that the witness
needs special protection.

131
00:09:34,679 --> 00:09:36,198
I'm given to understand

132
00:09:36,199 --> 00:09:39,358
that this witness
is not only not a minor,

133
00:09:39,359 --> 00:09:40,958
she is in fact a call girl.

134
00:09:40,959 --> 00:09:42,232
She's a whore.

135
00:09:45,359 --> 00:09:49,558
Mr Michaels, does your client,
in his wildest dreams,

136
00:09:49,559 --> 00:09:52,278
imagine that this
can possibly help his case?

137
00:09:52,279 --> 00:09:55,552
I apologise, my lord, on his behalf.

138
00:09:56,399 --> 00:09:57,798
Thank you, Mr Michaels.

139
00:09:57,799 --> 00:10:02,238
Mrs Mills, is there a good reason why
she should be shielded in this way?

140
00:10:02,239 --> 00:10:05,598
Is she of a particularly
nervous disposition?

141
00:10:05,599 --> 00:10:09,158
She is very nervous, my lord,
having already been threatened.

142
00:10:09,159 --> 00:10:10,479
Not by us.

143
00:10:11,359 --> 00:10:12,996
It was by someone,

144
00:10:12,999 --> 00:10:17,878
and that caused the police to
offer her a place in witness protection.

145
00:10:17,879 --> 00:10:19,238
As she is under police protection,

146
00:10:19,239 --> 00:10:24,558
I am reluctant to allow her to be
examined or cross-examined via CCTV.

147
00:10:24,559 --> 00:10:27,478
I do think the defendants
have a right to face their accuser.

148
00:10:27,479 --> 00:10:28,718
Damn straight, they do.

149
00:10:28,719 --> 00:10:32,318
However, that privilege
will be denied you, Mr Nicolaides,

150
00:10:32,319 --> 00:10:36,398
unless you remain silent,
and if you don't I will have you removed

151
00:10:36,399 --> 00:10:39,158
and put into cells below this court
for the duration of the trial.

152
00:10:39,159 --> 00:10:40,918
Hey, look. I haven't done nothing.

153
00:10:40,919 --> 00:10:43,838
That is what we're here to find out.

154
00:10:43,839 --> 00:10:46,358
My lord, this is a witness
who agreed to give evidence

155
00:10:46,359 --> 00:10:49,189
if her anonymity was protected.

156
00:10:49,279 --> 00:10:52,638
That is not acceptable
in this court, Mrs Mills.

157
00:10:52,639 --> 00:10:55,878
If she is not protected,
she is likely to take flight.

158
00:10:55,879 --> 00:10:58,438
Well, you said she was
in witness protection.

159
00:10:58,439 --> 00:11:00,638
If she flees,
she'll be held in contempt.

160
00:11:00,639 --> 00:11:02,718
That will only compound the problem.

161
00:11:02,719 --> 00:11:04,901
Mrs Mills, I have ruled.

162
00:11:06,399 --> 00:11:08,945
No. No, I'm not gonna do it.

163
00:11:10,079 --> 00:11:12,638
I'm not gonna give evidence
against them. Why should I?

164
00:11:12,639 --> 00:11:14,598
Because you saw them
kill that young man.

165
00:11:14,599 --> 00:11:17,998
He wasn't a man. He was a drug dealer.

166
00:11:17,999 --> 00:11:20,398
They'll kill me.
Why should I die for him?

167
00:11:20,399 --> 00:11:22,878
If you give evidence against them,
the chances are they'll go to prison.

168
00:11:22,879 --> 00:11:24,918
Then their friends will kill me.

169
00:11:24,919 --> 00:11:28,398
- Do you think anyone cares about me?
- Reb, I care. The court cares.

170
00:11:28,399 --> 00:11:30,399
They'll still kill me.

171
00:11:37,599 --> 00:11:38,789
George.

172
00:11:43,239 --> 00:11:47,558
- Neil. Have you been drinking?
- I'm intoxicated with power.

173
00:11:47,559 --> 00:11:49,398
I've just come from
a meeting with the Prime Minister.

174
00:11:49,399 --> 00:11:51,678
It can't be that
he's found you out at last.

175
00:11:51,679 --> 00:11:54,318
He asked me to become Home Secretary.

176
00:11:54,319 --> 00:11:56,798
I accepted.
He thinks I'll be tough on offenders.

177
00:11:56,799 --> 00:12:00,678
Do you think that's wise?
It might be a poisoned chalice.

178
00:12:00,679 --> 00:12:03,043
George, will you marry me?

179
00:12:03,319 --> 00:12:04,718
Is that a condition of the job?

180
00:12:04,719 --> 00:12:06,278
It wouldn't do me any harm
to have a wife.

181
00:12:06,279 --> 00:12:10,461
- Any wife?
- No, George. You. What do you say?

182
00:12:13,399 --> 00:12:15,109
Congratulations.

183
00:12:24,919 --> 00:12:25,949
Hello?

184
00:12:26,039 --> 00:12:27,798
Is that a good mood or a bad mood?

185
00:12:27,799 --> 00:12:29,669
Um, good, I think.

186
00:12:30,719 --> 00:12:32,398
Depends what you want. Where are you?

187
00:12:32,399 --> 00:12:36,118
I'm by my car waiting
for a wheel clamp to be removed.

188
00:12:36,119 --> 00:12:38,958
I don't remove wheel clamps, George.

189
00:12:38,959 --> 00:12:41,278
Can't you ask your boyfriend
to use his influence?

190
00:12:41,279 --> 00:12:44,358
I expect he could.
He's just been made Home Secretary.

191
00:12:44,359 --> 00:12:46,269
You can't be serious.

192
00:12:46,559 --> 00:12:50,078
I'm in need of some
urgent advice about him.

193
00:12:50,079 --> 00:12:52,716
I don't know who else to ask.

194
00:12:52,719 --> 00:12:58,118
I'm about 15 minutes from your flat
once this clamp gets removed.

195
00:12:58,119 --> 00:12:59,229
Um...

196
00:13:00,399 --> 00:13:03,218
I'll be there before you, then.

197
00:13:07,319 --> 00:13:08,774
Come on.

198
00:13:10,719 --> 00:13:13,558
Mrs Cooper,
is it possible to see the judge?

199
00:13:13,559 --> 00:13:15,832
I can ask him, Mrs Mills.

200
00:13:22,879 --> 00:13:26,398
- Is defence counsel with her?
- I don't think so, Judge.

201
00:13:26,399 --> 00:13:28,309
Then I can't see her.

202
00:13:28,439 --> 00:13:32,349
In a case like this,
it could spell trouble.

203
00:13:39,279 --> 00:13:44,518
I have this awful feeling
Neil has been behaving corruptly.

204
00:13:44,519 --> 00:13:46,238
So he did get your clamp off?

205
00:13:46,239 --> 00:13:48,989
No. Nothing so useful.

206
00:13:49,079 --> 00:13:50,807
George, he's an MP.

207
00:13:51,039 --> 00:13:54,998
Worse yet, now he's Home Secretary.
And he's very rich.

208
00:13:54,999 --> 00:13:57,678
So he must have
exploited or robbed somebody.

209
00:13:57,679 --> 00:13:59,358
That's just your prejudice.

210
00:13:59,359 --> 00:14:01,518
Well, he didn't win the lottery, did he?

211
00:14:01,519 --> 00:14:03,518
At least not the one
most of us play each week.

212
00:14:03,519 --> 00:14:06,389
He made another fortune

213
00:14:06,479 --> 00:14:10,389
trading shares in
the One-way Phone Company.

214
00:14:10,679 --> 00:14:13,861
- He had inside information.
- Oooh.

215
00:14:14,479 --> 00:14:16,638
If he did and he gets found out,
he could go to prison.

216
00:14:16,639 --> 00:14:18,730
Now, there's a thought.

217
00:14:18,839 --> 00:14:21,198
The Home Secretary in jail.

218
00:14:21,199 --> 00:14:25,078
I don't want to be married to
an ex-prisoner or an ex-Home Secretary.

219
00:14:25,079 --> 00:14:27,958
I'm sure Neil
can take care of himself.

220
00:14:27,959 --> 00:14:30,869
It's Daddy I'm worried about.

221
00:14:31,679 --> 00:14:36,678
Neil put him onto the shares,
he made a whole chunk of mon.

222
00:14:36,679 --> 00:14:40,158
I thought One-way's shares
collapsed during the trial.

223
00:14:40,159 --> 00:14:43,078
Neil advised the government
not to support them.

224
00:14:43,079 --> 00:14:46,878
He sold a huge forward position, and
subsequently picked them up for a song

225
00:14:46,879 --> 00:14:47,958
as did Daddy.

226
00:14:47,959 --> 00:14:51,198
Are you saying that Joe knew
that lover boy had inside information?

227
00:14:51,199 --> 00:14:52,927
I don't know, John.

228
00:14:53,119 --> 00:14:55,238
It's not the sort of question
one asks one's father.

229
00:14:55,239 --> 00:14:59,421
- "Are you behaving corruptly?"
- I'd ask mine.

230
00:15:01,759 --> 00:15:06,305
- John, you keep looking at my legs.
- Do I? Sorry.

231
00:15:07,599 --> 00:15:13,278
- They're still great legs.
- What are we going to do about Daddy?

232
00:15:13,279 --> 00:15:15,078
Well, unless Joe knew in advance

233
00:15:15,079 --> 00:15:18,278
that Haughton was going to persuade
the government not to support One-way,

234
00:15:18,279 --> 00:15:20,078
he hasn't broken the law, has he?

235
00:15:20,079 --> 00:15:21,798
Daddy wouldn't have
sold the shares if he did.

236
00:15:21,799 --> 00:15:24,163
Neil, on the other hand...

237
00:15:25,839 --> 00:15:29,438
All right, why did
he give Joe the information?

238
00:15:29,439 --> 00:15:31,478
My guess is that he was
looking for some insurance

239
00:15:31,479 --> 00:15:34,078
in case things went wrong for him.

240
00:15:34,079 --> 00:15:36,807
No. He wouldn't be so devious.

241
00:15:37,119 --> 00:15:39,483
George. He's a politician.

242
00:15:43,439 --> 00:15:46,429
What are you thinking?

243
00:15:53,359 --> 00:15:56,038
I understand the problem, Mrs Mills.

244
00:15:56,039 --> 00:15:59,078
Most prospective parents get nervous
about going before the adoption panel.

245
00:15:59,079 --> 00:16:01,318
No, it's nothing to do with that.

246
00:16:01,319 --> 00:16:03,998
It would help me if I could
put it off until I'm out of court.

247
00:16:03,999 --> 00:16:06,363
It's a tricky prosecution.

248
00:16:06,479 --> 00:16:08,518
We've heard that before.

249
00:16:08,519 --> 00:16:10,918
You said your previous case
would be your last.

250
00:16:10,919 --> 00:16:15,374
I had to do this one.
The CPS briefed me ages ago.

251
00:16:15,959 --> 00:16:18,918
Well, first and foremost,
the panel will want to know

252
00:16:18,919 --> 00:16:21,198
how you'll cope with Michael.

253
00:16:21,199 --> 00:16:24,438
And I have to say in all fairness,
Mrs Mills, I don't think you will.

254
00:16:24,439 --> 00:16:27,118
I think you're too committed
to your career.

255
00:16:27,119 --> 00:16:31,518
And for that reason,
I've recommended you're not suitable.

256
00:16:31,519 --> 00:16:32,709
What?

257
00:16:35,159 --> 00:16:38,198
But I thought you were crying out
for people to adopt older children.

258
00:16:38,199 --> 00:16:40,718
People with more stable lives
than yours seems to be.

259
00:16:40,719 --> 00:16:42,901
I work. Parents do work.

260
00:16:42,959 --> 00:16:45,718
Of course.
But there has to be some consistency.

261
00:16:45,719 --> 00:16:48,758
Now, twice last month
you cancelled seeing Michael.

262
00:16:48,759 --> 00:16:50,358
He was very upset.

263
00:16:50,359 --> 00:16:53,814
I did explain to him. I felt terrible.

264
00:16:54,879 --> 00:16:59,789
How do you think Michael
will feel if I give up on him?

265
00:17:11,279 --> 00:17:13,709
- Hello?
- John, it's Jo.

266
00:17:14,479 --> 00:17:16,398
Well, this is surprise.

267
00:17:16,399 --> 00:17:17,879
Can I come up?

268
00:17:18,999 --> 00:17:20,818
Yeah, push the door.

269
00:17:36,839 --> 00:17:38,558
Well, here's a situation.

270
00:17:38,559 --> 00:17:41,038
Well, if you'd have
spared me a few minutes in chambers

271
00:17:41,039 --> 00:17:42,358
to discuss this witness, John,

272
00:17:42,359 --> 00:17:43,918
I would have spared you
this embarrassment.

273
00:17:43,919 --> 00:17:47,558
Jo, you're before me. I can't see you.
Especially not here.

274
00:17:47,559 --> 00:17:50,014
Why? Is there someone here?

275
00:17:55,759 --> 00:17:57,958
Jo, I came to get John's advice on...

276
00:17:57,959 --> 00:18:00,718
Yeah, I'm sure that's not
all you got, George.

277
00:18:00,719 --> 00:18:03,356
What's that supposed to mean?

278
00:18:23,119 --> 00:18:26,158
- Aren't I before you?
- Can I come in?

279
00:18:26,159 --> 00:18:28,878
- Should you be here, my lord?
- No, certainly should not.

280
00:18:28,879 --> 00:18:31,358
Well, how nice that you have
such sensibilities for the judge,

281
00:18:31,359 --> 00:18:32,918
and none for the main witness.

282
00:18:32,919 --> 00:18:34,598
Could that be because
she's a mere prostitute,

283
00:18:34,599 --> 00:18:36,078
and as such has got to be punished?

284
00:18:36,079 --> 00:18:37,958
Don't be childish, Jo,
I've never behaved like that.

285
00:18:37,959 --> 00:18:41,478
You saw what the defendants were like,
they were even trying it on with you.

286
00:18:41,479 --> 00:18:44,389
- I beg you to think again.
- No.

287
00:18:46,759 --> 00:18:49,198
Perhaps you'd like to go a step further?

288
00:18:49,199 --> 00:18:51,718
Let's have a trial without a jury
and without witnesses.

289
00:18:51,719 --> 00:18:54,158
- That is ridiculous.
- It could happen.

290
00:18:54,159 --> 00:18:57,638
If we persistently chip away at
the safeguards to a public legal system,

291
00:18:57,639 --> 00:18:59,229
it could happen.

292
00:18:59,319 --> 00:19:04,138
This trial has to be open,
and fair, and seen as such.

293
00:19:04,879 --> 00:19:05,989
Fine.

294
00:19:18,759 --> 00:19:19,789
Jo...

295
00:19:19,879 --> 00:19:22,718
Social Services are giving me
a hard time over adopting Michael

296
00:19:22,719 --> 00:19:24,538
because I'm working.

297
00:19:25,319 --> 00:19:27,838
Then you are between
a rock and a hard place.

298
00:19:27,839 --> 00:19:31,198
I've been giving you a hard time because
you're thinking of stopping work.

299
00:19:31,199 --> 00:19:33,478
They're recommending
the adoption committee turns me down.

300
00:19:33,479 --> 00:19:36,638
Jo, that's outrageous.
You'd make a wonderful mother.

301
00:19:36,639 --> 00:19:40,458
Precisely because
you work and have a life.

302
00:19:42,519 --> 00:19:44,974
I do so hate you sometimes.

303
00:19:46,439 --> 00:19:47,759
Do you?

304
00:19:49,279 --> 00:19:50,759
Do you really?

305
00:20:03,919 --> 00:20:05,829
Excuse me, Judge.

306
00:20:06,039 --> 00:20:09,838
The jury usher informs me we haven't
got enough candidates to empanel a jury.

307
00:20:09,839 --> 00:20:10,918
Why is that?

308
00:20:10,919 --> 00:20:13,398
Too many middle-class people
managed to get out of jury duty.

309
00:20:13,399 --> 00:20:15,518
Without a jury system,
their middle-class existence

310
00:20:15,519 --> 00:20:17,558
- would be threatened, wouldn't it?
- Absolutely.

311
00:20:17,559 --> 00:20:20,558
Somebody better hop down to
the Job Centre and round up some bodies.

312
00:20:20,559 --> 00:20:22,758
- Can we do that, my lord?
- Certainly can.

313
00:20:22,759 --> 00:20:26,238
Under the 1974 Juries Act,
we can require any person to be a juror.

314
00:20:26,239 --> 00:20:30,478
It's called, for your information,
praying a tales.

315
00:20:30,479 --> 00:20:31,799
All right.

316
00:20:33,679 --> 00:20:39,318
The Lord Chief Justice is absolutely
furious about the whole jury issue.

317
00:20:39,319 --> 00:20:42,358
We should have fought a little harder
when those idiots in government

318
00:20:42,359 --> 00:20:45,398
were depriving us
of our civil liberties.

319
00:20:45,399 --> 00:20:48,438
A bit like bolting
the stable door now, isn't it?

320
00:20:48,439 --> 00:20:52,478
I think the thin end of the wedge is
a more appropriate metaphor.

321
00:20:52,479 --> 00:20:55,038
Are there any further changes
in the pipeline?

322
00:20:55,039 --> 00:20:58,798
Yes. The new Home Secretary
wants to test the water

323
00:20:58,799 --> 00:21:01,254
with a few non-jury trials.

324
00:21:01,399 --> 00:21:07,318
Bad enough when you were my son-in-law.
This one is a complete disaster.

325
00:21:07,319 --> 00:21:11,278
Is that something you are about to eat
or have already eaten?

326
00:21:11,279 --> 00:21:13,478
It's tofu stir-fry, my lord.

327
00:21:13,479 --> 00:21:17,278
- Tofu? What is it, Japanese?
- Chinese, I believe, my lord.

328
00:21:17,279 --> 00:21:19,552
It's made from bean curd.

329
00:21:21,679 --> 00:21:24,198
The jury issue might be
out of my hands by now anyway,

330
00:21:24,199 --> 00:21:26,038
I can't get enough
bodies to empanel them.

331
00:21:26,039 --> 00:21:29,598
Well, under Section 16
you can sit with as few as nine jurors.

332
00:21:29,599 --> 00:21:32,598
I'm surprised by your resistance, Joe.

333
00:21:32,599 --> 00:21:36,078
I'd have thought that your experience
on the Appellate Bench

334
00:21:36,079 --> 00:21:42,358
would have shown you just how very
short of useful juries sometimes are.

335
00:21:42,359 --> 00:21:46,678
In my present mood I'm not prepared
to accommodate the Lord Chancellor,

336
00:21:46,679 --> 00:21:51,438
or whatever he may call himself
in his new department.

337
00:21:51,439 --> 00:21:54,158
I'm certainly not
going to accommodate Neil Haughton.

338
00:21:54,159 --> 00:21:58,149
Well, actually, this is rather good.

339
00:22:00,319 --> 00:22:01,469
Is it?

340
00:22:03,279 --> 00:22:06,958
I do apologise for the way
that you've been press-ganged,

341
00:22:06,959 --> 00:22:08,758
but it is a matter of grave urgency.

342
00:22:08,759 --> 00:22:11,358
The liberty of the three men
in the dock is at stake.

343
00:22:11,359 --> 00:22:14,198
- I wanna object to some of the jurors.
- Why is that, Mr Balfe?

344
00:22:14,199 --> 00:22:18,654
Check them, man.
They're all white bitches, innit?

345
00:22:19,399 --> 00:22:24,069
Mr Balfe, I will not
tolerate racism in any form.

346
00:22:24,599 --> 00:22:26,558
How am I gonna get
no fair trial from Babylon?

347
00:22:26,559 --> 00:22:27,678
I can see where you're going with this.

348
00:22:27,679 --> 00:22:29,798
You will not force me
to abandon this trial.

349
00:22:29,799 --> 00:22:33,436
Mr Harrison, swear
the entire jury panel.

350
00:22:34,239 --> 00:22:36,278
What, all 15 of them, my lord?

351
00:22:36,279 --> 00:22:39,158
It would be sensible, wouldn't it,
to adopt the American system

352
00:22:39,159 --> 00:22:41,318
and have three in reserve?

353
00:22:41,319 --> 00:22:46,958
Though, I dare say Mr Mason Allen
would give me an argument about that.

354
00:22:46,959 --> 00:22:52,398
My lord, I'd hesitate to argue
with Your Lordship on any numbers,

355
00:22:52,399 --> 00:22:57,389
but I believe the Juries Act of 1825

356
00:22:57,479 --> 00:23:00,478
requires only 12 jurors to be sworn.

357
00:23:00,479 --> 00:23:03,878
Yes, Mr Mason Allen,
Section 25 of the Act did,

358
00:23:03,879 --> 00:23:07,998
but it was repealed in 1972
by the Criminal Justice Act,

359
00:23:07,999 --> 00:23:12,398
which neglected to state
how many jurors should be sworn.

360
00:23:12,399 --> 00:23:14,189
It only assumed 12.

361
00:23:18,159 --> 00:23:21,278
- Swear 12, Mr Harrison.
- Yes, my lord.

362
00:23:21,279 --> 00:23:23,643
May I have you in, please?

363
00:23:29,599 --> 00:23:32,878
Take the Bible in your hand,
either hand, and read from the card.

364
00:23:32,879 --> 00:23:36,598
"I swear by Almighty God that I will
faithfully try the defendants

365
00:23:36,599 --> 00:23:41,236
"and give a true verdict
according to the evidence."

366
00:23:53,039 --> 00:23:55,998
Jo, our main witness hasn't shown up.

367
00:23:55,999 --> 00:23:59,198
- Didn't the police bring her?
- She won't come out of the house.

368
00:23:59,199 --> 00:24:00,758
Didn't they tell her that the only way

369
00:24:00,759 --> 00:24:03,758
that she'll get free is
to put that lot in prison?

370
00:24:03,759 --> 00:24:07,850
It's their family
and friends she's afraid of.

371
00:24:13,959 --> 00:24:17,358
- How do you propose to get her to court?
- By appealing to her common sense.

372
00:24:17,359 --> 00:24:19,198
In the meantime,
I'll change the order of the witnesses

373
00:24:19,199 --> 00:24:21,563
and call the police first.

374
00:24:21,639 --> 00:24:23,198
All right. Let's get on.

375
00:24:23,199 --> 00:24:24,789
Inspector wyman,

376
00:24:25,079 --> 00:24:27,918
when you were called
to the Sunset Hotel,

377
00:24:27,919 --> 00:24:29,078
what did you find?

378
00:24:29,079 --> 00:24:33,158
I found a young oriental man dead
on the floor of one of the rooms.

379
00:24:33,159 --> 00:24:36,038
He had two small bullet holes
in his chest.

380
00:24:36,039 --> 00:24:38,438
A pizza had been squashed on his face.

381
00:24:38,439 --> 00:24:41,918
- Had he been eating the pizza?
- The pizza was more or less intact.

382
00:24:41,919 --> 00:24:43,638
There was none
in the contents of his stomach.

383
00:24:43,639 --> 00:24:45,838
Were you able to identify this man?

384
00:24:45,839 --> 00:24:48,878
From the hotel register,
we identified him as Simon Jackson.

385
00:24:48,879 --> 00:24:51,198
This was later confirmed
by his fingerprints.

386
00:24:51,199 --> 00:24:53,678
He was known to the police
as a drugs dealer.

387
00:24:53,679 --> 00:24:55,838
His turf was in Harlesden.

388
00:24:55,839 --> 00:24:57,798
He was part of a gang called No Problem,

389
00:24:57,799 --> 00:25:01,478
which was at the time at war with
another outfit called The Trigger.

390
00:25:01,479 --> 00:25:06,318
Do you know or are you able to identify
any of the rival gang, The Trigger?

391
00:25:06,319 --> 00:25:07,389
Yes.

392
00:25:07,479 --> 00:25:11,118
Each of the three defendants
are members of The Trigger.

393
00:25:11,119 --> 00:25:14,718
And there are at least another two
on the public seating in court today.

394
00:25:14,719 --> 00:25:17,838
Well, as long as they're not
doing anything to disrupt this trial,

395
00:25:17,839 --> 00:25:20,476
they have a right to be here.

396
00:25:21,519 --> 00:25:23,998
Did you find anything else
at the hotel, Inspector?

397
00:25:23,999 --> 00:25:27,038
A lady's handbag was propped
on a shelf behind the toilet.

398
00:25:27,039 --> 00:25:30,278
We identified it as belonging
to one Rebecca Spark.

399
00:25:30,279 --> 00:25:32,158
At first she claimed
she'd had it stolen.

400
00:25:32,159 --> 00:25:33,398
Did she report it stolen?

401
00:25:33,399 --> 00:25:35,998
No. And when we told her we found
her fingerprints in the hotel room,

402
00:25:35,999 --> 00:25:39,278
she broke down,
said she'd seen the shooting.

403
00:25:39,279 --> 00:25:41,518
Was she able to identify the killers?

404
00:25:41,519 --> 00:25:42,718
She gave us a description

405
00:25:42,719 --> 00:25:45,758
and later picked each of the three
defendants out from photographs,

406
00:25:45,759 --> 00:25:48,718
then from a videoed
police identity parade.

407
00:25:48,719 --> 00:25:51,538
Was there any doubt about that?

408
00:25:51,599 --> 00:25:54,758
At first she was reluctant
to identify them even on video,

409
00:25:54,759 --> 00:25:57,718
the girl was terrified
that she'd be next.

410
00:25:57,719 --> 00:26:00,356
She took a lot of reassuring.

411
00:26:01,559 --> 00:26:05,158
Can you tell us again
how you identified Rebecca Spark?

412
00:26:05,159 --> 00:26:07,558
From her fingerprints
found in the hotel.

413
00:26:07,559 --> 00:26:12,438
So, when you went to interview her
and she said her bag had been stolen,

414
00:26:12,439 --> 00:26:14,798
you took her fingerprints,

415
00:26:14,799 --> 00:26:18,118
as you presumably do
with all victims of crime.

416
00:26:18,119 --> 00:26:20,878
No. Her fingerprints were on record.

417
00:26:20,879 --> 00:26:24,789
She has a criminal record.

418
00:26:24,879 --> 00:26:26,389
For selling it.

419
00:26:27,159 --> 00:26:28,379
Mr Balfe.

420
00:26:28,479 --> 00:26:30,878
She has a police record.

421
00:26:30,879 --> 00:26:33,238
She has criminal convictions?

422
00:26:33,239 --> 00:26:34,309
Yes.

423
00:26:36,439 --> 00:26:38,029
Well, Inspector,

424
00:26:38,839 --> 00:26:41,198
do we have to drag this out
like drawing teeth?

425
00:26:41,199 --> 00:26:42,558
She has five convictions,

426
00:26:42,559 --> 00:26:46,598
two for shoplifting, one for
selling drugs, two for prostitution.

427
00:26:46,599 --> 00:26:49,319
And how old is Miss Spark?

428
00:26:50,079 --> 00:26:54,534
- 19, sir.
- Quite a career for a 19-year-old.

429
00:26:56,879 --> 00:27:00,958
Did you establish whether she was
known either to the dead man

430
00:27:00,959 --> 00:27:04,414
or the defendants prior to the murder?

431
00:27:04,519 --> 00:27:06,358
She was known to the victim, sir.

432
00:27:06,359 --> 00:27:11,358
Did she sell drugs for him
and work for him as a prostitute?

433
00:27:11,359 --> 00:27:13,318
That is what her record reflects.

434
00:27:13,319 --> 00:27:15,501
I see.

435
00:27:15,879 --> 00:27:20,879
Mr Mason Allen, just a moment, please.
Rudkin, stand up.

436
00:27:21,559 --> 00:27:24,118
Get your hands out of your pockets.

437
00:27:24,119 --> 00:27:26,210
Do you know that juror?

438
00:27:27,439 --> 00:27:28,659
No, sir.

439
00:27:29,999 --> 00:27:32,198
You seemed to be gesturing to him.
Were you?

440
00:27:32,199 --> 00:27:35,109
- No.
- Sky, you behave yourself.

441
00:27:36,239 --> 00:27:39,598
If you signal or
make threatening gestures to this jury,

442
00:27:39,599 --> 00:27:43,238
I will bounce you down to the cells,
make no mistake about that.

443
00:27:43,239 --> 00:27:44,509
Sit down.

444
00:27:46,639 --> 00:27:47,909
Mr Mason.

445
00:27:53,319 --> 00:27:56,749
Inspector. During this investigation,

446
00:27:57,319 --> 00:28:01,518
were there ever any other suspects
apart from the three defendants?

447
00:28:01,519 --> 00:28:02,549
No, sir.

448
00:28:02,639 --> 00:28:07,038
So, once they were identified,
you went all out to nail them.

449
00:28:07,039 --> 00:28:09,758
No, we proceeded
to corroborate the evidence.

450
00:28:09,759 --> 00:28:13,278
Did you never once consider
it might be someone else?

451
00:28:13,279 --> 00:28:15,038
The evidence suggested not.

452
00:28:15,039 --> 00:28:18,198
Did you not consider
the sex worker herself?

453
00:28:18,199 --> 00:28:22,438
Simon Jackson had led her into a life
of crime and prostitution, after all.

454
00:28:22,439 --> 00:28:23,798
No, sir. We didn't.

455
00:28:23,799 --> 00:28:26,958
So, as soon as Miss Spark
said they were black,

456
00:28:26,959 --> 00:28:29,798
you decided it was
Mr Balfe and his friends.

457
00:28:29,799 --> 00:28:31,598
Mr Nicolaides isn't black, sir.

458
00:28:31,599 --> 00:28:35,158
You have run across Mr Balfe a number
of times and failed to convict him.

459
00:28:35,159 --> 00:28:36,398
Miss Spark picked him out.

460
00:28:36,399 --> 00:28:40,398
If this prostitute hadn't identified
each of these three young men,

461
00:28:40,399 --> 00:28:42,478
is it possible you would have considered

462
00:28:42,479 --> 00:28:47,934
the girl, or her lover,
or her brother, or father as suspect?

463
00:28:48,839 --> 00:28:50,567
It's possible, sir.

464
00:28:57,599 --> 00:29:00,398
What's up with you, bruv?
This court's already found us guilty.

465
00:29:00,399 --> 00:29:02,478
It's not doing us any favours acting up.

466
00:29:02,479 --> 00:29:04,198
That's your old man's dog-collar talk.

467
00:29:04,199 --> 00:29:08,238
The only way we're gonna walk
is if that jury goes down.

468
00:29:08,239 --> 00:29:11,758
You're either with us or against us.
What is it?

469
00:29:11,759 --> 00:29:13,269
You know, Jez.

470
00:29:15,479 --> 00:29:16,749
You know.

471
00:29:25,119 --> 00:29:28,318
Judge, I've just had
the jury bailiff in.

472
00:29:28,319 --> 00:29:31,078
The jury are feeling menaced by
the defendants and their families.

473
00:29:31,079 --> 00:29:33,438
Some don't want to go back into court.

474
00:29:33,439 --> 00:29:36,318
All right, I'll talk to defence counsel
about giving the jury protection.

475
00:29:36,319 --> 00:29:37,998
Tell counsel I'll see them in chambers.

476
00:29:37,999 --> 00:29:39,029
Okay.

477
00:29:40,319 --> 00:29:42,798
I can't reasonably object to protection

478
00:29:42,799 --> 00:29:46,278
if the jury really
are feeling pressured.

479
00:29:46,279 --> 00:29:51,279
These defendants do seem to
think they're above the law.

480
00:29:51,319 --> 00:29:54,078
They aren't doing their cause any good.

481
00:29:54,079 --> 00:29:56,625
I'll point that out to them.

482
00:29:57,759 --> 00:30:02,718
A public trial is an important plank
of a free and democratic society.

483
00:30:02,719 --> 00:30:05,718
To have a trial without either
the public or the defendants present

484
00:30:05,719 --> 00:30:08,447
would reflect badly on us all.

485
00:30:08,919 --> 00:30:11,278
But if there is any more pressure
put on this jury,

486
00:30:11,279 --> 00:30:16,916
either by look or gesture,
I will clear the court and the dock.

487
00:30:16,919 --> 00:30:19,438
Unless you are quite
without intelligence,

488
00:30:19,439 --> 00:30:22,998
you must know that you can't
be doing your cause any good.

489
00:30:22,999 --> 00:30:27,038
The jurors are only human,
and you are likely to prejudice them.

490
00:30:27,039 --> 00:30:29,518
Now, if you think
this is going to get you a retrial

491
00:30:29,519 --> 00:30:32,598
or aid any appeal
in the event of a guilty verdict,

492
00:30:32,599 --> 00:30:34,478
then I must tell you
that the appeal court

493
00:30:34,479 --> 00:30:36,598
will read the transcript of this trial,

494
00:30:36,599 --> 00:30:40,509
and they will see
that you have been warned.

495
00:30:40,839 --> 00:30:43,238
In any event, I will inquire
of the jury at the end

496
00:30:43,239 --> 00:30:49,149
to see if they've been influenced
by events in the public gallery.

497
00:30:52,879 --> 00:30:55,198
Okay, let's have the jury back.

498
00:30:55,199 --> 00:30:56,349
Judge,

499
00:30:56,639 --> 00:31:00,038
Sussex police are refusing
to provide protection for the jury

500
00:31:00,039 --> 00:31:02,278
because the defendants are
London overspill.

501
00:31:02,279 --> 00:31:04,038
They want the DCA to agree to pay.

502
00:31:04,039 --> 00:31:06,949
- And the DCA's refusing?
- Yeah.

503
00:31:12,639 --> 00:31:16,958
It isn't that the department refuses
to pay for police protection, Sir John.

504
00:31:16,959 --> 00:31:20,478
It's a cost that must be borne
by Sussex or the Home Office.

505
00:31:20,479 --> 00:31:24,358
What about using some of the extra money
the CPS has for witness protection?

506
00:31:24,359 --> 00:31:26,718
That's for witness protection.

507
00:31:26,719 --> 00:31:29,478
Anyway, the Home Office say
it's all spent.

508
00:31:29,479 --> 00:31:33,398
Is this a means of bringing pressure
in the quest to abandon jury trials?

509
00:31:33,399 --> 00:31:35,598
Would you sooner criminals went free?

510
00:31:35,599 --> 00:31:38,798
Rather than embrace
the idea of a single judge trial, yes.

511
00:31:38,799 --> 00:31:40,518
The prospect is repugnant.

512
00:31:40,519 --> 00:31:42,718
It's an economic reality.

513
00:31:42,719 --> 00:31:46,758
One the new Home Secretary
is keen to drive through.

514
00:31:46,759 --> 00:31:49,238
The criminal justice budget
can't go on expanding,

515
00:31:49,239 --> 00:31:52,118
as much as we might
argue for it to do so.

516
00:31:52,119 --> 00:31:54,318
Well, the solution is simple, Ian.

517
00:31:54,319 --> 00:31:58,398
The government must legislate
for us all to be good and honest.

518
00:31:58,399 --> 00:32:00,518
I think there's
a Criminal Justice bill on its way

519
00:32:00,519 --> 00:32:02,610
that will do just that.

520
00:32:02,959 --> 00:32:05,678
Meanwhile, you have
a predominantly white jury

521
00:32:05,679 --> 00:32:08,678
trying predominantly black defendants.

522
00:32:08,679 --> 00:32:10,549
Hardly their peers.

523
00:32:14,079 --> 00:32:17,443
Two of the jurors have reported sick.

524
00:32:18,519 --> 00:32:21,610
- Is it a bug of some sort?
- Yeah.

525
00:32:26,799 --> 00:32:30,598
Two jurors falling
ill is very inconvenient.

526
00:32:30,599 --> 00:32:35,518
I could stop the trial and start again,
but then the same problem might arise.

527
00:32:35,519 --> 00:32:37,429
Then abandon it.

528
00:32:38,959 --> 00:32:42,505
Who said that? Come on, who called out?

529
00:32:45,039 --> 00:32:47,398
- What's your name?
- Bea waxman.

530
00:32:47,399 --> 00:32:50,358
Well, Bea waxman, are you related
to any of the defendants?

531
00:32:50,359 --> 00:32:52,558
Yes, sir. I'm Dave's sister.

532
00:32:52,559 --> 00:32:54,198
Dave's sister, you can either

533
00:32:54,199 --> 00:32:56,478
apologise to the court
for your interruption,

534
00:32:56,479 --> 00:32:58,278
or go down to a cell for contempt.

535
00:32:58,279 --> 00:33:00,718
- He didn't do nothing.
- It seems you've chosen the cell.

536
00:33:00,719 --> 00:33:04,447
Would a police officer
remove her, please?

537
00:33:08,439 --> 00:33:10,439
She didn't do nothing.

538
00:33:10,959 --> 00:33:13,278
Seems to be a trait in your family,
Mr Nicolaides.

539
00:33:13,279 --> 00:33:15,461
Now shut it or join her.

540
00:33:16,999 --> 00:33:18,269
Sit down.

541
00:33:21,039 --> 00:33:23,838
We will continue
the trial with 10 jurors.

542
00:33:23,839 --> 00:33:27,476
- You can't do that.
- I can't, Mr Balfe?

543
00:33:27,879 --> 00:33:30,038
You'll see what I can do.

544
00:33:30,039 --> 00:33:33,358
See how it is?
My man's already got his mind made up.

545
00:33:33,359 --> 00:33:38,278
Again, I hesitate in arguing
this point with you, my lord,

546
00:33:38,279 --> 00:33:43,638
but isn't there a danger
of going forward with only 10 jurors?

547
00:33:43,639 --> 00:33:46,185
I don't think so. Mrs Mills.

548
00:33:48,119 --> 00:33:50,574
I'd like to call Reb Spark.

549
00:33:50,879 --> 00:33:54,478
Miss Spark, would you tell the court
what happened in the hotel room

550
00:33:54,479 --> 00:33:56,798
on the 19th of September last?

551
00:33:56,799 --> 00:33:59,518
What, you mean after Sime
had sex or before?

552
00:33:59,519 --> 00:34:00,869
No, during it.

553
00:34:02,079 --> 00:34:05,558
- Dock officer, take him down.
- Don't touch me, man.

554
00:34:05,559 --> 00:34:07,069
Don't touch me.

555
00:34:12,799 --> 00:34:16,890
- Mrs Mills.
- Miss Spark, after will be fine.

556
00:34:18,679 --> 00:34:22,358
I was in the bathroom
getting ready to go to work.

557
00:34:22,359 --> 00:34:25,478
And...
There was a knock at the door.

558
00:34:25,479 --> 00:34:27,838
Three men came in
when Sime went and opened it.

559
00:34:27,839 --> 00:34:29,509
Are they in court?

560
00:34:32,959 --> 00:34:34,669
Two of them are.

561
00:34:34,839 --> 00:34:36,598
This might be painful for you,

562
00:34:36,599 --> 00:34:41,418
but I'm not sure
that anyone but me heard your answer.

563
00:34:43,959 --> 00:34:47,323
Two of them are. The two in the dock.

564
00:34:48,079 --> 00:34:49,349
Stand up.

565
00:34:50,559 --> 00:34:51,959
Both of you.

566
00:34:54,959 --> 00:34:57,141
Do you know their names?

567
00:34:58,759 --> 00:34:59,869
Yeah.

568
00:35:01,559 --> 00:35:05,014
He's Jesus Balfe. And he's Sky Rudkin.

569
00:35:05,439 --> 00:35:09,478
The third one was the one what
was taken out. Dave Nicolaides.

570
00:35:09,479 --> 00:35:11,758
They shot Sime and
squashed a pizza on his face.

571
00:35:11,759 --> 00:35:13,578
Maybe he was hungry.

572
00:35:15,039 --> 00:35:17,585
Dock officer, take him down.

573
00:35:19,239 --> 00:35:22,589
Hands off me, man. Yeah. Yeah.

574
00:35:27,439 --> 00:35:31,349
Miss Spark, how did
the shooting come about?

575
00:35:32,399 --> 00:35:34,036
They were arguing.

576
00:35:34,039 --> 00:35:38,078
Saying that Sime was nicking their turf
and he was a dead man.

577
00:35:38,079 --> 00:35:42,358
Jesus Balfe pulled a gun out
and give it to him, Sky.

578
00:35:42,359 --> 00:35:46,905
Dave told him to ventilate him,
but he didn't move.

579
00:35:46,999 --> 00:35:48,318
He was shaking.

580
00:35:48,319 --> 00:35:52,238
He didn't want to do it
and Sime was shouting.

581
00:35:52,239 --> 00:35:55,718
Then Jez grabbed the gun back
and shot him.

582
00:35:55,719 --> 00:35:59,174
What happened after Mr Balfe shot him?

583
00:35:59,599 --> 00:36:03,327
The white one smacked a pizza
in his face.

584
00:36:04,719 --> 00:36:08,992
He said, "That's on us, dog."
Then they ran out.

585
00:36:12,279 --> 00:36:13,869
I waited a while

586
00:36:14,519 --> 00:36:16,338
and then I ran, too.

587
00:36:20,599 --> 00:36:24,798
You killed Sime Jackson, that well-known
pimp and drugs dealer, didn't you?

588
00:36:24,799 --> 00:36:25,869
No.

589
00:36:25,959 --> 00:36:29,918
He asked me there
to have sex with him to celebrate.

590
00:36:29,919 --> 00:36:31,838
You charge men for having sex.

591
00:36:31,839 --> 00:36:34,567
So what if I do? It's my body.

592
00:36:34,959 --> 00:36:37,118
Most of the money you earned

593
00:36:37,119 --> 00:36:40,392
you gave to Sime Jackson. Your pimp.

594
00:36:40,639 --> 00:36:43,549
He was your pimp, wasn't he?

595
00:36:43,639 --> 00:36:47,238
I gave him money
for drugs and protection, that's all.

596
00:36:47,239 --> 00:36:49,278
Isn't it true, Miss Spark,

597
00:36:49,279 --> 00:36:53,358
that you were a sex slave to a pimp
who got you hooked on drugs?

598
00:36:53,359 --> 00:36:56,758
You went to the hotel room and killed
him as the only means of getting free.

599
00:36:56,759 --> 00:36:59,198
No. I didn't. I saw them do it.

600
00:36:59,199 --> 00:37:04,238
Did you not say to Bel Robinson,
another of Jackson's sex slaves,

601
00:37:04,239 --> 00:37:08,878
that the only way to get free
of that man was to shoot him?

602
00:37:08,879 --> 00:37:11,518
What? I didn't mean it. I didn't.

603
00:37:11,519 --> 00:37:14,638
But Mr Jackson
ended up shot dead just the same.

604
00:37:14,639 --> 00:37:16,038
Is that coincidence?

605
00:37:16,039 --> 00:37:17,678
You can't say that I did it.

606
00:37:17,679 --> 00:37:21,316
I didn't.
I don't have to listen to this.

607
00:37:27,639 --> 00:37:29,189
Reb. Reb, wait.

608
00:37:30,679 --> 00:37:32,798
If you run like that,
the judge could get seriously annoyed.

609
00:37:32,799 --> 00:37:36,518
- Yeah? What's he gonna do?
- He could stick you in jail.

610
00:37:36,519 --> 00:37:38,238
You're all against me in there.

611
00:37:38,239 --> 00:37:40,398
No one believes me.
I might as well be dead.

612
00:37:40,399 --> 00:37:41,909
We believe you.

613
00:37:42,879 --> 00:37:44,029
We do.

614
00:37:45,839 --> 00:37:47,059
Come on.

615
00:38:09,079 --> 00:38:11,278
It's doing my nut in
being cooped up in here the whole time.

616
00:38:11,279 --> 00:38:12,398
You'll be fine.

617
00:38:12,399 --> 00:38:14,238
When you're done
they'll get a whacking sentence.

618
00:38:14,239 --> 00:38:16,158
I can't stand it here
with nothing to do.

619
00:38:16,159 --> 00:38:18,398
I just wanna go and have a good time.

620
00:38:18,399 --> 00:38:19,549
Look,

621
00:38:20,439 --> 00:38:23,530
I'll go and get you some new DVDs.

622
00:39:04,559 --> 00:39:09,287
I can't take you with me
because I've got a hot date.

623
00:39:10,639 --> 00:39:14,638
Don't look like that.
Two girls just don't work for me, Mimi.

624
00:39:14,639 --> 00:39:15,859
Come on.

625
00:39:16,199 --> 00:39:19,199
It's okay. Come on. Night, night.

626
00:39:24,239 --> 00:39:26,421
- Jo?
- Porter let me up.

627
00:39:26,639 --> 00:39:27,998
You obviously haven't heard.

628
00:39:27,999 --> 00:39:33,363
Reb Spark was killed this afternoon
by a hit-and-run driver.

629
00:39:37,199 --> 00:39:39,118
How? She had police protection.

630
00:39:39,119 --> 00:39:40,958
It seems she gave them the slip.

631
00:39:40,959 --> 00:39:43,238
If you would have let her
give evidence via a video link,

632
00:39:43,239 --> 00:39:44,718
she would still be alive.

633
00:39:44,719 --> 00:39:46,629
Yes, yes. Yes, I'm...

634
00:39:51,159 --> 00:39:53,318
Are the police treating it as murder?

635
00:39:53,319 --> 00:39:57,558
The driver didn't just knock her down.
He reversed over her.

636
00:39:57,559 --> 00:39:59,358
You judged her for being a prostitute.

637
00:39:59,359 --> 00:40:05,541
You decided that she wasn't worth
as much as your precious principle.

638
00:40:06,079 --> 00:40:07,429
Well, um...

639
00:40:09,279 --> 00:40:11,638
Obviously I can't go on hearing the case
under the circumstances...

640
00:40:11,639 --> 00:40:13,821
John, another principle.

641
00:40:13,919 --> 00:40:17,358
If you stop the trial now,
her evidence will be gone forever.

642
00:40:17,359 --> 00:40:20,996
That young woman will have died in vain.

643
00:40:37,799 --> 00:40:38,998
This is John Deed.

644
00:40:38,999 --> 00:40:43,118
I can't come to the phone.
Please leave me a message.

645
00:40:43,119 --> 00:40:45,238
John, it's George.

646
00:40:45,239 --> 00:40:49,694
I assume you're on your way.
I'll try your mobile.

647
00:40:56,559 --> 00:41:01,469
Because of the death
of the prosecution's main witness,

648
00:41:01,759 --> 00:41:03,358
I'm going to adjourn for the day

649
00:41:03,359 --> 00:41:06,878
to allow the prosecution
to reorganise its witnesses.

650
00:41:06,879 --> 00:41:10,698
- We all get a holiday.
- What did you say?

651
00:41:11,639 --> 00:41:12,909
Stand up.

652
00:41:18,079 --> 00:41:19,559
Apologise now.

653
00:41:20,839 --> 00:41:23,203
What? I didn't do nothing.

654
00:41:24,999 --> 00:41:26,818
You won't apologise?

655
00:41:27,199 --> 00:41:29,518
You can remain in the cell
below the court until you do.

656
00:41:29,519 --> 00:41:30,999
Take him down.

657
00:41:56,119 --> 00:41:57,709
Are you unhappy?

658
00:42:00,759 --> 00:42:04,958
- I'm going to give up the law.
- But being a barrister's good.

659
00:42:04,959 --> 00:42:06,279
Sometimes.

660
00:42:07,039 --> 00:42:10,958
Sometimes you have to deal with
such horrid things and such

661
00:42:10,959 --> 00:42:12,549
wretched people.

662
00:42:13,679 --> 00:42:16,498
You begin to feel contaminated.

663
00:42:16,919 --> 00:42:19,278
It would be much nicer
looking after you.

664
00:42:19,279 --> 00:42:21,158
Will you come
to my house for the weekend?

665
00:42:21,159 --> 00:42:24,523
Will you come and get me?

666
00:42:32,359 --> 00:42:36,038
What do you want
done with Mr Balfe, Judge?

667
00:42:36,039 --> 00:42:37,838
Is he still in the cell?

668
00:42:37,839 --> 00:42:42,758
The van's waiting to take
the others back to the remand centre.

669
00:42:42,759 --> 00:42:44,518
Leave Balfe where he is.

670
00:42:44,519 --> 00:42:47,478
Good. He deserves
solitary confinement for life.

671
00:42:47,479 --> 00:42:50,518
I hope that's what you give him, Judge.

672
00:42:50,519 --> 00:42:52,998
Coop, would you ask the Chief Constable
to come and see me?

673
00:42:52,999 --> 00:42:59,181
No, order the Chief Constable
to come and see me in the digs, please?

674
00:43:06,359 --> 00:43:12,087
- What about Jez?
- Don't worry about him.

675
00:43:13,399 --> 00:43:14,829
What is this?

676
00:43:15,919 --> 00:43:17,139
Come on.

677
00:43:38,999 --> 00:43:41,158
Are you close
to finding Miss Spark's killer?

678
00:43:41,159 --> 00:43:46,432
We found the stolen security van.
It was dumped and burned.

679
00:43:47,279 --> 00:43:48,638
Any clues to the driver?

680
00:43:48,639 --> 00:43:52,078
The van was caught twice
on CCTV cameras near the court.

681
00:43:52,079 --> 00:43:53,989
The driver is a blur.

682
00:43:54,159 --> 00:43:57,432
We are trying to enhance the images.

683
00:43:59,599 --> 00:44:01,958
We are checking every camera in town.

684
00:44:01,959 --> 00:44:03,758
I want round-the-clock protection

685
00:44:03,759 --> 00:44:05,398
on each of the other main witnesses
and the jury.

686
00:44:05,399 --> 00:44:09,036
I don't care who pays for it,
just do it.

687
00:44:32,359 --> 00:44:33,549
Jo.

688
00:44:35,079 --> 00:44:38,807
- Now Terry Post has gone missing.
- What?

689
00:44:54,759 --> 00:44:56,718
What's happened to your next witness,
Mrs Mills?

690
00:44:56,719 --> 00:44:59,438
It seems he's scared, my lord.

691
00:44:59,439 --> 00:45:02,598
He doesn't want what happened
to Miss Spark to happen to him.

692
00:45:02,599 --> 00:45:04,758
I thought he had
round-the-clock protection.

693
00:45:04,759 --> 00:45:06,578
Supposedly, my lord.

694
00:45:07,119 --> 00:45:09,118
Mr Post disappeared through
the toilet window

695
00:45:09,119 --> 00:45:11,847
at the back of his pizza shop.

696
00:45:12,799 --> 00:45:14,389
Mr Harrison?

697
00:45:14,479 --> 00:45:15,718
Contact the Chief Constable.

698
00:45:15,719 --> 00:45:17,638
Tell him I want Mr Post found
and brought to court.

699
00:45:17,639 --> 00:45:19,358
- Yes, sir.
- Can you proceed without him?

700
00:45:19,359 --> 00:45:22,078
- My next witness is here.
- Good, let's have the jury in.

701
00:45:22,079 --> 00:45:26,518
My lord, it seems two more jury members
have reported sick.

702
00:45:26,519 --> 00:45:29,429
- Doctors' notes?
- Yes, my lord.

703
00:45:31,319 --> 00:45:34,918
- They're from the same doctor.
- Yes, my lord.

704
00:45:34,919 --> 00:45:37,278
I want the doctor brought to court
to explain himself.

705
00:45:37,279 --> 00:45:38,869
All rise.

706
00:45:44,159 --> 00:45:46,678
Sir Ian Rochester, my lord.
And lunch is ready.

707
00:45:46,679 --> 00:45:48,718
- Thank you, Mrs Johnson. Ian.
- Sir John.

708
00:45:48,719 --> 00:45:52,447
- Do you want some lunch?
- No, thank you.

709
00:45:52,919 --> 00:45:55,998
I heard your trial's falling apart.

710
00:45:55,999 --> 00:46:00,598
Mightn't this be an appropriate case for
you to continue hearing without a jury?

711
00:46:00,599 --> 00:46:02,838
That means the terrorists win, Ian.

712
00:46:02,839 --> 00:46:07,158
I understood from Laurence James that
this was a drugs-related murder trial.

713
00:46:07,159 --> 00:46:10,678
Someone is terrorising
both the jury and the witnesses.

714
00:46:10,679 --> 00:46:14,558
If it takes the intervention
of the trial judge to shore up the jury,

715
00:46:14,559 --> 00:46:17,798
and half the Sussex police force
to protect them,

716
00:46:17,799 --> 00:46:20,598
is the convention worth fighting for?

717
00:46:20,599 --> 00:46:22,958
Ajudge without a jury
worked in Northern Ireland.

718
00:46:22,959 --> 00:46:25,438
Nobody ever believed it was just.

719
00:46:25,439 --> 00:46:29,558
It would solve our increasing
economic dilemma.

720
00:46:29,559 --> 00:46:32,238
So too would extending the powers
of summary justice

721
00:46:32,239 --> 00:46:35,998
to policemen and traffic wardens.
We could go beyond on-the-spot fines.

722
00:46:35,999 --> 00:46:39,398
The hands of thieves and the fingers
of pickpockets could be lopped off.

723
00:46:39,399 --> 00:46:42,198
We could do away with
the rule of law altogether.

724
00:46:42,199 --> 00:46:45,038
I wouldn't suggest that
to this Home Secretary.

725
00:46:45,039 --> 00:46:47,585
He might find it attractive.

726
00:46:48,639 --> 00:46:51,358
Dr woods, did you actually
examine these jurors?

727
00:46:51,359 --> 00:46:54,838
I don't issue sick notes
without examining the patient.

728
00:46:54,839 --> 00:46:56,238
They're suffering from
the same complaint?

729
00:46:56,239 --> 00:46:58,069
Yes, from stress.

730
00:46:58,159 --> 00:47:00,518
Everybody finds jury service stressful.

731
00:47:00,519 --> 00:47:04,358
One has a number of abrasions.
He wouldn't say how he got them.

732
00:47:04,359 --> 00:47:07,269
He appeared to have been beaten.

733
00:47:09,839 --> 00:47:11,029
I see.

734
00:47:11,759 --> 00:47:15,578
Yeah, thank you, Doctor,
you can step down.

735
00:47:17,319 --> 00:47:20,309
I'm going to adjourn again

736
00:47:20,399 --> 00:47:23,358
so I can visit these jurors
in their homes myself.

737
00:47:23,359 --> 00:47:26,038
- My lord...
- Yes, I know. Highly irregular.

738
00:47:26,039 --> 00:47:28,118
I would invite counsel to accompany me,

739
00:47:28,119 --> 00:47:30,358
but in this case I think
it would prove counter-productive.

740
00:47:30,359 --> 00:47:34,878
In any case, a transcript will be made
available to the court.

741
00:47:34,879 --> 00:47:36,334
All rise.

742
00:47:37,599 --> 00:47:40,236
Were you beaten up, Mr Arrow?

743
00:47:40,559 --> 00:47:42,509
I tripped over, sir.

744
00:47:44,799 --> 00:47:48,078
Now, how did that happen,
with the police protecting you?

745
00:47:48,079 --> 00:47:49,559
I don't know.

746
00:47:50,239 --> 00:47:51,559
Thank you.

747
00:47:52,799 --> 00:47:57,890
Could you identify whoever it was
who caused you to trip?

748
00:48:02,119 --> 00:48:06,998
Sometimes we have to be very courageous
to do what we know is right.

749
00:48:06,999 --> 00:48:10,478
If we give in to the threats
of the bully, the strong man,

750
00:48:10,479 --> 00:48:13,570
or the terrorist, everybody loses.

751
00:48:13,599 --> 00:48:17,398
Countless people through the centuries
have suffered torture,

752
00:48:17,399 --> 00:48:21,589
deprivation, loss of life,
saying no to him.

753
00:48:21,679 --> 00:48:27,278
That young witness gave her life
doing what she thought was right.

754
00:48:27,279 --> 00:48:30,038
Now, I'm not saying
that she did it willingly.

755
00:48:30,039 --> 00:48:33,278
In fact, I'm quite certain that she
would rather have done anything

756
00:48:33,279 --> 00:48:35,518
than come into court
and do what she did.

757
00:48:35,519 --> 00:48:37,998
But having done it,
we have to ask ourselves,

758
00:48:37,999 --> 00:48:42,181
"Are we going to let her sacrifice
be in vain?"

759
00:48:45,999 --> 00:48:51,318
I can't force you to go back into court.
Your doctor says you're ill.

760
00:48:51,319 --> 00:48:55,229
But I can't continue
with only eight jurors.

761
00:49:00,999 --> 00:49:03,429
So, thanks for the tea.

762
00:49:13,159 --> 00:49:15,718
The jury bailiff said
they didn't show up, Judge.

763
00:49:15,719 --> 00:49:18,719
He's still got only eight jurors.

764
00:49:19,399 --> 00:49:21,438
Ask counsel to come
to chambers please, Coop.

765
00:49:21,439 --> 00:49:26,598
In spite of my best endeavours,
I still haven't got enough jurors.

766
00:49:26,599 --> 00:49:29,958
Now, the options are to adjourn
until the jurors are well enough...

767
00:49:29,959 --> 00:49:33,918
- And, if they're not, Judge?
- Abandon the trial.

768
00:49:33,919 --> 00:49:35,109
No.

769
00:49:36,399 --> 00:49:39,438
Now, if I was to do that, Jo,
can the CPS remount it?

770
00:49:39,439 --> 00:49:43,158
Unlikely, with our main witness dead.

771
00:49:43,159 --> 00:49:45,318
The Secretary of State
for Constitutional Affairs,

772
00:49:45,319 --> 00:49:46,878
driven, of course,
by the Home Secretary,

773
00:49:46,879 --> 00:49:50,516
would like me to hear it without a jury.

774
00:49:50,639 --> 00:49:54,918
My client would never agree.
Even if the law were in place.

775
00:49:54,919 --> 00:49:56,998
- Nor mine.
- You can't do that, John.

776
00:49:56,999 --> 00:49:59,078
They seem to think that I can.

777
00:49:59,079 --> 00:50:02,038
Under Section 44
of the 2003 Criminal Justice Act.

778
00:50:02,039 --> 00:50:04,758
And even if I can't,
they're sure to amend it.

779
00:50:04,759 --> 00:50:06,318
It flies in the face of justice.

780
00:50:06,319 --> 00:50:11,410
I agree. But somehow,
these three have got to face trial.

781
00:50:14,439 --> 00:50:17,998
One option would be
to continue with eight jurors.

782
00:50:17,999 --> 00:50:20,438
My lord, that would be unsafe.

783
00:50:20,439 --> 00:50:22,518
Convictions have been upheld with nine.

784
00:50:22,519 --> 00:50:25,238
With respect, John, eight is not nine.

785
00:50:25,239 --> 00:50:29,158
Yes, I agree, but it's preferable
to abandoning the trial.

786
00:50:29,159 --> 00:50:32,159
Or hearing it with a judge alone.

787
00:50:39,439 --> 00:50:41,149
I don't know.

788
00:50:44,399 --> 00:50:47,678
I'm going to adjourn
and seek wiser counsel.

789
00:50:47,679 --> 00:50:51,589
My clerk will let you know
when we next sit.

790
00:50:55,719 --> 00:50:57,229
Come on, then.

791
00:50:58,279 --> 00:51:01,238
I won't let these three young men
escape without a trial, Joe.

792
00:51:01,239 --> 00:51:03,558
Is this because you feel
somehow responsible

793
00:51:03,559 --> 00:51:06,650
for the death of this young woman?

794
00:51:08,839 --> 00:51:11,678
No, I'm afraid you must
abandon this trial.

795
00:51:11,679 --> 00:51:14,958
If they were to be convicted
with only eight jurors,

796
00:51:14,959 --> 00:51:16,638
you would certainly be overturned.

797
00:51:16,639 --> 00:51:20,238
Why are nine jurors
less fallible than eight?

798
00:51:20,239 --> 00:51:22,798
Well, they're not, in theory.

799
00:51:22,799 --> 00:51:25,798
But what if murder were still
a capital offence?

800
00:51:25,799 --> 00:51:27,398
Putting young men away for 30 years

801
00:51:27,399 --> 00:51:29,158
is not something that
I'd undertake lightly,

802
00:51:29,159 --> 00:51:31,278
however many jurors convicted them.

803
00:51:31,279 --> 00:51:33,158
If you want these bods to stand trial,

804
00:51:33,159 --> 00:51:36,078
you must be prepared
to go ahead without a jury.

805
00:51:36,079 --> 00:51:38,638
Even though it's an affront
to civil liberties.

806
00:51:38,639 --> 00:51:39,789
Joe...

807
00:51:42,239 --> 00:51:45,603
I have to ask you something personal.

808
00:51:46,879 --> 00:51:51,198
You sold a lot of shares in a forward
position in the One-way Phone Company?

809
00:51:51,199 --> 00:51:54,563
Yes, I did, yes. Made a lot of money.

810
00:51:55,239 --> 00:51:57,598
Why did you trade those shares?

811
00:51:57,599 --> 00:52:00,509
Neil Haughton put me on to them.

812
00:52:01,519 --> 00:52:04,247
Why, is there something wrong?

813
00:52:04,439 --> 00:52:07,358
There might be.
What did Haughton tell you?

814
00:52:07,359 --> 00:52:11,909
He told me that One-way
were under pressure,

815
00:52:11,999 --> 00:52:16,878
and that selling a forward position
could be advantageous.

816
00:52:16,879 --> 00:52:19,238
When he was at the Department of Trade,

817
00:52:19,239 --> 00:52:23,078
Haughton advised the government
not to support One-way.

818
00:52:23,079 --> 00:52:26,478
He knew the share price was going down.

819
00:52:26,479 --> 00:52:30,198
- Yes, but that could look bad for me.
- Yes, it could.

820
00:52:30,199 --> 00:52:33,718
If somebody decided to go after
our new Home Secretary,

821
00:52:33,719 --> 00:52:37,038
and then he reached out to you for help.

822
00:52:37,039 --> 00:52:40,221
Is somebody likely to go after him?

823
00:52:51,799 --> 00:52:53,958
I suppose you'd be very pleased
if I am forced

824
00:52:53,959 --> 00:52:56,918
into conducting a trial without a jury.

825
00:52:56,919 --> 00:52:58,878
That would suit
the Home Office very well.

826
00:52:58,879 --> 00:53:02,278
We have to find some way to get on top
of the legions of offenders.

827
00:53:02,279 --> 00:53:04,558
I have no intention
of setting that precedent.

828
00:53:04,559 --> 00:53:07,118
John, we live in a society where 90%
of crimes go unpunished.

829
00:53:07,119 --> 00:53:11,598
Better that 99% go unpunished
than one person is convicted wrongly.

830
00:53:11,599 --> 00:53:14,878
You wouldn't take that view
if you had to answer to the electorate.

831
00:53:14,879 --> 00:53:16,998
I answer to my conscience.

832
00:53:16,999 --> 00:53:20,358
The voters of this country
are fed up to their back teeth

833
00:53:20,359 --> 00:53:22,238
of criminals playing the system.

834
00:53:22,239 --> 00:53:26,279
Winning because juries
are afraid to convict.

835
00:53:26,359 --> 00:53:28,638
You won't make them unafraid
by abandoning them.

836
00:53:28,639 --> 00:53:31,998
You might not have any regard
for the voters, I can't ignore them.

837
00:53:31,999 --> 00:53:33,758
It's the voters
who will lose out in the long run

838
00:53:33,759 --> 00:53:35,358
if you unpick their safeguards.

839
00:53:35,359 --> 00:53:40,078
John, you must want to see
wrongdoers convicted, surely.

840
00:53:40,079 --> 00:53:42,829
Yes, but justly, fairly.

841
00:53:43,439 --> 00:53:45,678
Then we're on the same side.

842
00:53:45,679 --> 00:53:49,358
Necessity sometimes
makes strange bedfellows.

843
00:53:49,359 --> 00:53:53,178
As does conspiracy
to defraud shareholders.

844
00:53:53,279 --> 00:53:54,759
- What?
- John.

845
00:53:55,559 --> 00:53:57,118
- I've conspired with no one.
- Haven't you?

846
00:53:57,119 --> 00:53:59,756
Let me get you another drink.

847
00:54:03,639 --> 00:54:06,549
I will not run a juryless trial.

848
00:54:07,879 --> 00:54:09,958
If you're not prepared
to abandon your principles,

849
00:54:09,959 --> 00:54:13,198
then you'll have to abandon the trial,
let these boys go.

850
00:54:13,199 --> 00:54:14,869
George. Must dash.

851
00:54:58,399 --> 00:55:00,581
- Hello.
- Jo, it's John.

852
00:55:26,639 --> 00:55:31,639
I can't find an echo of a precedent
for a jury of eight.

853
00:55:33,679 --> 00:55:39,238
Did you mean it when you said
I was responsible for that girl's death?

854
00:55:39,239 --> 00:55:41,678
We say things in the heat of the moment.

855
00:55:41,679 --> 00:55:43,478
Yeah, tell me about it.

856
00:55:43,479 --> 00:55:46,198
I came that close to hitting
Neil Haughton this evening.

857
00:55:46,199 --> 00:55:48,654
Won't bring her back, John.

858
00:55:48,999 --> 00:55:51,878
I will not sanction a juryless trial.

859
00:55:51,879 --> 00:55:56,078
Fine, then the defendants get to walk.
That's for your conscience.

860
00:55:56,079 --> 00:55:58,989
I have a meeting to prepare for.

861
00:56:02,559 --> 00:56:05,798
How would you describe
your lifestyle, Mrs Mills?

862
00:56:05,799 --> 00:56:08,345
Busy, but getting less busy.

863
00:56:08,359 --> 00:56:12,398
Isn't the life of a leading criminal QC
permanently busy?

864
00:56:12,399 --> 00:56:14,318
I plan to take a sabbatical.

865
00:56:14,319 --> 00:56:16,918
Does that mean you'd become busy again
in a year or so?

866
00:56:16,919 --> 00:56:20,838
No, I'd give up work to find
a workable routine for Michael.

867
00:56:20,839 --> 00:56:23,918
Yes, but that does sound like you intend
to get busy again, Jo.

868
00:56:23,919 --> 00:56:28,465
It's not my plan to give up
working forever, Simon.

869
00:56:28,479 --> 00:56:32,558
After all, you manage to sit on this
committee as well as being a busy QC.

870
00:56:32,559 --> 00:56:35,318
- Adoption is a big commitment.
- Yes.

871
00:56:35,319 --> 00:56:37,838
Children are for life,
not just a sabbatical.

872
00:56:37,839 --> 00:56:41,112
Yes, do you think I don't know that?

873
00:56:41,599 --> 00:56:48,236
I did bring up two children on my own
after their father got ill and died.

874
00:56:49,759 --> 00:56:53,123
Yes, of course, Mrs Mills. I'm sorry.

875
00:56:54,319 --> 00:56:58,238
Are you sure you're not looking
to adopt in order to fill the gap

876
00:56:58,239 --> 00:57:00,038
left by your children going to college?

877
00:57:00,039 --> 00:57:02,758
Michael's mother asked me
to take him when she was dying.

878
00:57:02,759 --> 00:57:07,118
You didn't pursue this request
immediately after Miss Hulsey's death.

879
00:57:07,119 --> 00:57:10,029
No. I'm sorry I didn't.

880
00:57:10,999 --> 00:57:13,829
I didn't think I could cope.

881
00:57:13,919 --> 00:57:17,638
It took a lot of soul-searching
on my part.

882
00:57:17,639 --> 00:57:19,798
But it's precisely because I put myself
through this process

883
00:57:19,799 --> 00:57:21,981
that I feel so sure now.

884
00:57:22,279 --> 00:57:24,758
Mightn't it be better
if you simply befriended Michael

885
00:57:24,759 --> 00:57:26,918
in some other situation altogether?

886
00:57:26,919 --> 00:57:29,238
Well, what's going to happen to him?

887
00:57:29,239 --> 00:57:32,598
We'd look for another foster placement.

888
00:57:32,599 --> 00:57:34,198
Are you completely stupid?

889
00:57:34,199 --> 00:57:37,078
How on earth
will that be better for him?

890
00:57:37,079 --> 00:57:40,078
Here you have someone who wants him,
and who loves him,

891
00:57:40,079 --> 00:57:44,078
even if she can't spend 24 hours
a day with him.

892
00:57:44,079 --> 00:57:48,261
I'm sorry.
We're turning down your application.

893
00:57:55,919 --> 00:57:56,989
Jo.

894
00:58:17,279 --> 00:58:19,309
Hello? Jo?

895
00:58:40,879 --> 00:58:42,879
It's a tough call, Jo.

896
00:58:53,599 --> 00:58:55,781
Maybe it's for the best.

897
00:58:57,839 --> 00:59:01,930
I'm still not convinced
that you're convinced.

898
00:59:02,599 --> 00:59:08,145
I promised Michael he could
come home with me. I promised him.

899
00:59:09,359 --> 00:59:11,198
Have they got
a better alternative for him?

900
00:59:11,199 --> 00:59:17,290
Help me, John. I want to smash
this panel with its arbitrary powers.

901
00:59:17,439 --> 00:59:19,189
Are they arbitrary?

902
00:59:20,759 --> 00:59:24,758
You could go for judicial review
on their decision.

903
00:59:24,759 --> 00:59:31,214
But that will take weeks. Months.
Michael will think I've abandoned him.

904
00:59:36,959 --> 00:59:39,596
Are you ready to give up law?

905
00:59:42,199 --> 00:59:46,563
Is it worth going on with?
You're no longer sure.

906
00:59:49,439 --> 00:59:54,038
- Have you solved your problem?
- No. I'm running out of time.

907
00:59:54,039 --> 00:59:58,403
But I'm not going to give up, Jo.
Nor should you.

908
01:00:00,399 --> 01:00:02,718
You can be a mother and a lawyer.

909
01:00:02,719 --> 01:00:04,810
Panel doesn't think so.

910
01:00:04,999 --> 01:00:09,718
They want a perfect world for children
to be adopted into. It isn't possible.

911
01:00:09,719 --> 01:00:13,901
Yes, well, you could argue that
before a judge.

912
01:00:14,079 --> 01:00:15,518
You can't wait for a perfect world

913
01:00:15,519 --> 01:00:18,798
according to the diktats
of some petty bureaucrat.

914
01:00:18,799 --> 01:00:22,078
Nor should you be expected
to create a perfect world.

915
01:00:22,079 --> 01:00:27,170
You'll do the best you can.
That's all any parent can do.

916
01:00:29,519 --> 01:00:33,589
You've been a wonderful single mother.

917
01:00:35,639 --> 01:00:37,821
And you can do it again.

918
01:00:41,079 --> 01:00:42,299
I can?

919
01:00:43,639 --> 01:00:44,709
Yes.

920
01:00:49,719 --> 01:00:54,174
I don't want you to lose your place
on the ladder.

921
01:00:54,839 --> 01:01:01,294
But that doesn't mean that you can't
have what you've set your heart on.

922
01:01:01,839 --> 01:01:03,658
Bless you, John.

923
01:01:13,799 --> 01:01:16,549
Mimi. John.

924
01:01:17,679 --> 01:01:21,198
Have you found the means
to repulse the barbarians?

925
01:01:21,199 --> 01:01:23,109
Still looking, Monty.

926
01:01:25,919 --> 01:01:29,838
Have you got time to fit in
an urgent judicial review application?

927
01:01:29,839 --> 01:01:33,638
I could, if the Administrative Court
agrees, and it's not too complicated.

928
01:01:33,639 --> 01:01:36,912
No, not much law, just common sense.

929
01:01:36,919 --> 01:01:39,478
We've got plenty of that.
Who's making the application?

930
01:01:39,479 --> 01:01:41,469
Jo Mills.

931
01:01:42,439 --> 01:01:46,638
Well, I'm not sure that I should
hear her, considering our history.

932
01:01:46,639 --> 01:01:49,598
You're probably the best person
because of it, Monty.

933
01:01:49,599 --> 01:01:51,038
Do you think so?

934
01:01:51,039 --> 01:01:55,318
Well, I'll ask my clerk
to look after the application.

935
01:01:55,319 --> 01:01:58,069
And John. If there's

936
01:01:58,959 --> 01:02:02,558
any way that I can support you
over the jury issue,

937
01:02:02,559 --> 01:02:04,589
- I will.
- Thanks.

938
01:02:10,319 --> 01:02:13,149
Jo. I'm glad I caught you.

939
01:02:14,719 --> 01:02:17,878
John suggested,
in the spirit of reconciliation,

940
01:02:17,879 --> 01:02:20,838
that I represent you
at this judicial review.

941
01:02:20,839 --> 01:02:26,958
- What? You can't be serious, George.
- He said you'd react like that.

942
01:02:26,959 --> 01:02:30,141
Well, that was my initial response.

943
01:02:30,319 --> 01:02:32,501
It's why I didn't phone.

944
01:02:33,199 --> 01:02:37,278
You'd be far better off with me.
I won't get over-emotional.

945
01:02:37,279 --> 01:02:40,734
- You think I will?
- You want the boy.

946
01:02:40,879 --> 01:02:42,698
His name is Michael.

947
01:02:43,279 --> 01:02:46,429
Yes, I know his name.

948
01:02:49,159 --> 01:02:51,887
Shall we go through the facts?

949
01:02:54,999 --> 01:02:58,779
Section 13
of the Child Adoption Act 1990,

950
01:02:58,879 --> 01:03:01,425
clearly states, and I quote,

951
01:03:01,719 --> 01:03:05,278
"A child of suitable age for adoption
should be placed

952
01:03:05,279 --> 01:03:09,038
"wherever possible with an adult
or adults of compatible ethnicity,

953
01:03:09,039 --> 01:03:12,838
"or any suitable adult, provided
they meet the minimum requirements

954
01:03:12,839 --> 01:03:15,021
"set out in Section 18."

955
01:03:15,879 --> 01:03:18,389
That, my lord, states that,

956
01:03:18,479 --> 01:03:22,958
"To be a responsible adult,
you must be over 18 years of age,

957
01:03:22,959 --> 01:03:26,158
"be of reasonable intelligence
and mental stability,

958
01:03:26,159 --> 01:03:28,238
"not have a criminal record,

959
01:03:28,239 --> 01:03:32,421
"and have some compatibility
with the adoptee."

960
01:03:43,119 --> 01:03:48,078
Mrs Mills meets the requirement
of the Act in every degree.

961
01:03:48,079 --> 01:03:52,118
More than that, she desires
to give Michael Hulsey

962
01:03:52,119 --> 01:03:53,798
a stable and loving home.

963
01:03:53,799 --> 01:03:58,527
Indeed, she promised his dying mother
that she would.

964
01:04:00,199 --> 01:04:02,678
The decision by the adoption committee
is not only arbitrary,

965
01:04:02,679 --> 01:04:07,952
it's contrary to both the spirit
and the letter of the Act.

966
01:04:09,599 --> 01:04:11,781
Thank you, Mrs Channing.

967
01:04:13,199 --> 01:04:14,549
Mr Norwalk.

968
01:04:15,519 --> 01:04:19,358
Collectively, the committee
that rejected Mrs Mills' application

969
01:04:19,359 --> 01:04:23,269
has been responsible
for over 600 adoptions.

970
01:04:24,679 --> 01:04:26,118
With their considerable experience,

971
01:04:26,119 --> 01:04:30,038
they judged her unsuitable
to adopt Michael Hulsey.

972
01:04:30,039 --> 01:04:31,438
Not least of all because they saw

973
01:04:31,439 --> 01:04:34,798
that she was a single mother
of adult teenagers,

974
01:04:34,799 --> 01:04:39,478
and a career lawyer who was not
about to step off the career ladder.

975
01:04:39,479 --> 01:04:43,558
Indeed, by her own admission, Mrs Mills
hopes one day to become a judge.

976
01:04:43,559 --> 01:04:47,278
Judges also can make
reasonable parents, Mr Norwalk.

977
01:04:47,279 --> 01:04:48,309
Indeed.

978
01:04:48,399 --> 01:04:50,838
Though it's a matter of interest,
Mr Norwalk,

979
01:04:50,839 --> 01:04:54,294
how many lesbians and homosexual males

980
01:04:54,479 --> 01:04:59,025
has this committee allowed
to adopt young children?

981
01:04:59,919 --> 01:05:04,598
With respect, my lord,
I cannot see that has any bearing.

982
01:05:04,599 --> 01:05:05,949
You can't?

983
01:05:06,519 --> 01:05:10,438
And yet, the same committee
that approved those adoptions

984
01:05:10,439 --> 01:05:15,118
deems someone unsuitable
if he or she works at a career.

985
01:05:15,119 --> 01:05:16,709
You astound me.

986
01:05:17,719 --> 01:05:20,719
I'll hear each of your arguments.

987
01:05:23,199 --> 01:05:27,118
...you have to consider,
while there's life, there's hope.

988
01:05:27,119 --> 01:05:28,519
Mrs Mills.

989
01:05:29,919 --> 01:05:32,158
Even if the committee
does review its decision,

990
01:05:32,159 --> 01:05:36,398
like the judge said it should,
it may come to the same conclusion.

991
01:05:36,399 --> 01:05:38,118
There's nothing the High Court
can do about that.

992
01:05:38,119 --> 01:05:43,038
No. But I would find a way
to overturn the committee. Somehow.

993
01:05:43,039 --> 01:05:45,078
You really are earnest, Mrs Mills.

994
01:05:45,079 --> 01:05:47,429
I won't give up. I can't.

995
01:05:48,599 --> 01:05:54,358
Well, perhaps you ought to have
a trial period with Michael at home.

996
01:05:54,359 --> 01:05:56,798
Does that mean
you're not going to oppose me?

997
01:05:56,799 --> 01:06:01,345
We're doing as the judge suggested.
Thinking again.

998
01:06:01,599 --> 01:06:02,919
A result.

999
01:06:10,639 --> 01:06:13,003
So, this can be your room.

1000
01:06:14,199 --> 01:06:16,678
You might want to put up
some different posters.

1001
01:06:16,679 --> 01:06:19,038
- Who's Meatloaf?
- They're a '70s pop group.

1002
01:06:19,039 --> 01:06:21,598
Mark has all sorts of tastes in music.

1003
01:06:21,599 --> 01:06:25,690
- Is that his cello?
- He gave it up.

1004
01:06:27,119 --> 01:06:30,918
Would you like to learn? It can be
great fun learning an instrument.

1005
01:06:30,919 --> 01:06:32,998
Mum used to play the recorder.

1006
01:06:32,999 --> 01:06:36,181
Well, would you like to learn that?

1007
01:06:40,759 --> 01:06:43,358
There's so much I've got to learn
about you, Michael.

1008
01:06:43,359 --> 01:06:48,450
- Like what time you go to bed.
- Mum said... She said...

1009
01:06:53,839 --> 01:06:57,203
we'll find our way, little by little.

1010
01:06:58,439 --> 01:07:01,349
Come on, let's make some supper.

1011
01:07:18,199 --> 01:07:22,638
Michael, what's the matter, darling?
Can't you sleep?

1012
01:07:22,639 --> 01:07:24,678
It's because it's a strange room,
I expect.

1013
01:07:24,679 --> 01:07:29,225
I keep thinking about Mum.
Why did she have to die?

1014
01:07:30,119 --> 01:07:31,519
I don't know.

1015
01:07:32,919 --> 01:07:34,349
No one does.

1016
01:07:36,279 --> 01:07:39,118
It's one of life's enduring mysteries.

1017
01:07:39,119 --> 01:07:43,198
Why someone as nice as your mum
has to die so young,

1018
01:07:43,199 --> 01:07:46,278
and someone who isn't very nice doesn't.

1019
01:07:46,279 --> 01:07:48,029
It was their fault.

1020
01:07:48,559 --> 01:07:51,378
They gave her the brain tumour.

1021
01:07:54,079 --> 01:07:56,598
Michael, come on. Come on, come on.

1022
01:07:56,599 --> 01:07:59,838
It's all right. It's all right.
It's all right.

1023
01:07:59,839 --> 01:08:03,558
I won't let anyone hurt you.
I'm here for you.

1024
01:08:03,559 --> 01:08:06,105
Have a good cry if you want.

1025
01:08:11,239 --> 01:08:13,239
There, it's all right.

1026
01:08:14,319 --> 01:08:19,918
Hello, this is Jo Mills. Please leave
a message and I'll call you back.

1027
01:08:19,919 --> 01:08:21,109
Jo...

1028
01:08:32,799 --> 01:08:35,072
what could you do anyway?

1029
01:08:48,079 --> 01:08:50,989
Mr Balfe has finally apologised.

1030
01:08:52,279 --> 01:08:56,758
Judge, you can't let these three
murderers walk away, Judge. You can't.

1031
01:08:56,759 --> 01:09:00,118
Coop, I can't allow civil liberties
to be eroded.

1032
01:09:00,119 --> 01:09:05,518
The right to a trial by a jury,
even for the most odious of killers,

1033
01:09:05,519 --> 01:09:07,158
is at the heart of our legal system.

1034
01:09:07,159 --> 01:09:10,918
- No, Judge...
- It's what makes us civilised.

1035
01:09:10,919 --> 01:09:13,798
People don't care about that, Judge.
They don't.

1036
01:09:13,799 --> 01:09:16,278
Not when their lives
are blighted by crime.

1037
01:09:16,279 --> 01:09:21,734
What they do care about is killers
getting to walk away free.

1038
01:09:47,679 --> 01:09:53,316
After a lot of deliberation
over whether to abandon this trial,

1039
01:09:56,879 --> 01:10:00,607
I have decided
to continue without a jury.

1040
01:10:00,759 --> 01:10:02,759
No, my lord. I mean...

1041
01:10:02,879 --> 01:10:07,318
I would like to register my protest
on behalf of my client.

1042
01:10:07,319 --> 01:10:09,118
And I on behalf of mine.

1043
01:10:09,119 --> 01:10:12,638
- You said this would be stopped.
- Sit down.

1044
01:10:12,639 --> 01:10:18,198
The idea of this trial continuing
without a jury is offensive, my lord.

1045
01:10:18,199 --> 01:10:19,909
As it is to me.

1046
01:10:20,799 --> 01:10:24,478
A greater injustice might be done to
these defendants, I hope you'll agree,

1047
01:10:24,479 --> 01:10:27,078
if we were to abandon it
without giving them the opportunity

1048
01:10:27,079 --> 01:10:30,118
to clear their names in open court.

1049
01:10:30,119 --> 01:10:32,598
Frankly, my lord, I don't think
any of us would agree.

1050
01:10:32,599 --> 01:10:35,158
My lord, two of our jurors
have recovered.

1051
01:10:35,159 --> 01:10:36,438
Are they here now?

1052
01:10:36,439 --> 01:10:40,530
In the jurors' assembly room,
with the others.

1053
01:10:41,639 --> 01:10:46,389
It appears that two of our jurors
have recovered.

1054
01:10:47,559 --> 01:10:48,958
So, let's have the jury back,

1055
01:10:48,959 --> 01:10:53,323
and continue with this trial
in a proper fashion.

1056
01:10:58,399 --> 01:11:00,438
In the absence of
the pizza-parlour owner,

1057
01:11:00,439 --> 01:11:02,798
I'd like to call Julia Peters,
a fingerprint expert.

1058
01:11:02,799 --> 01:11:06,358
- I thought the jury had fallen apart.
- We understood it had, completely.

1059
01:11:06,359 --> 01:11:07,758
It doesn't look like it, Mr James.

1060
01:11:07,759 --> 01:11:10,758
- There will be other opportunities.
- Not without further legislation.

1061
01:11:10,759 --> 01:11:15,214
Deed's resistance will encourage
the other judges.

1062
01:11:15,319 --> 01:11:19,118
There was a clear thumbprint
made up of all 18 ridges,

1063
01:11:19,119 --> 01:11:22,518
on the right-hand flap
of the pizza box lid.

1064
01:11:22,519 --> 01:11:24,558
Were you able to ascertain
whose print this was?

1065
01:11:24,559 --> 01:11:27,558
Yes. It belonged to one Jesus Balfe.

1066
01:11:27,559 --> 01:11:28,909
It's Jez.

1067
01:11:28,999 --> 01:11:33,758
Mr Balfe, if you don't shut up,
you'll be back in a cell.

1068
01:11:33,759 --> 01:11:38,759
Mrs Peters, is there any doubt
about whose print it was?

1069
01:11:38,799 --> 01:11:41,069
No, it was Mr Balfe's.

1070
01:11:41,159 --> 01:11:42,998
There is doubt, however, is there not,

1071
01:11:42,999 --> 01:11:46,598
that Mr Balfe put the pizza box
in the hotel room?

1072
01:11:46,599 --> 01:11:49,038
That's not my area of expertise.

1073
01:11:49,039 --> 01:11:52,438
So someone else could have taken
the box to the hotel room

1074
01:11:52,439 --> 01:11:56,109
after Mr Balfe had somehow touched it,

1075
01:11:56,199 --> 01:11:58,927
possibly in the pizza parlour.

1076
01:12:00,599 --> 01:12:01,789
I suppose.

1077
01:12:01,879 --> 01:12:03,970
Would you further agree

1078
01:12:04,199 --> 01:12:08,758
that Mr Balfe's thumbprint
on the box, at the hotel,

1079
01:12:08,759 --> 01:12:13,069
is not evidence
that he did the shooting?

1080
01:12:15,919 --> 01:12:19,478
My lord, my learned friend is trying
to get from this witness

1081
01:12:19,479 --> 01:12:21,398
what she is not qualified to answer.

1082
01:12:21,399 --> 01:12:22,998
I don't think he is, Mr Sorrell.

1083
01:12:22,999 --> 01:12:26,238
He's getting her to confirm
that she is not such an expert.

1084
01:12:26,239 --> 01:12:28,549
No, no. Mr Mason Allen.

1085
01:12:33,039 --> 01:12:34,439
Mrs Peters?

1086
01:12:35,159 --> 01:12:38,958
The thumbprint on the box
is not proof he did the shooting.

1087
01:12:38,959 --> 01:12:42,758
Then, by that token, not finding
Mr Nicolaides' prints on the box,

1088
01:12:42,759 --> 01:12:44,958
would indicate that
he didn't do the shooting.

1089
01:12:44,959 --> 01:12:49,238
Mr Michaels, aren't we straying
into Alice in Wonderland here?

1090
01:12:49,239 --> 01:12:54,694
I believe it is a favourite book
of most barristers, my lord.

1091
01:13:02,359 --> 01:13:04,638
My lord, the police have located
the pizza-parlour owner,

1092
01:13:04,639 --> 01:13:07,678
but Mr Post is not
being very cooperative.

1093
01:13:07,679 --> 01:13:09,718
I'd like to treat him as hostile.

1094
01:13:09,719 --> 01:13:11,269
Any objections?

1095
01:13:13,479 --> 01:13:14,829
Go on.

1096
01:13:15,799 --> 01:13:19,678
I don't recognise any of them.
I don't think they're my customers.

1097
01:13:19,679 --> 01:13:22,318
Then would you turn
to the witness bundle, Mr Post?

1098
01:13:22,319 --> 01:13:24,629
Item 19. Your statement.

1099
01:13:28,319 --> 01:13:31,229
Is that your name and signature?

1100
01:13:34,079 --> 01:13:36,078
Are you being threatened
by anyone, Mr Post?

1101
01:13:36,079 --> 01:13:38,079
Mrs Mills, be careful.

1102
01:13:40,999 --> 01:13:43,878
Mr Post, did you or did you not
tell the police

1103
01:13:43,879 --> 01:13:46,478
that the three defendants were
in your pizza parlour

1104
01:13:46,479 --> 01:13:50,025
prior to the shooting of Simon Jackson?

1105
01:13:51,119 --> 01:13:54,638
They can kill me if they want to.
If they do, my family will get them.

1106
01:13:54,639 --> 01:13:56,238
Mr Post, just answer the question.

1107
01:13:56,239 --> 01:13:58,878
Yes, they was there.
And they didn't pay for their pizzas.

1108
01:13:58,879 --> 01:14:00,918
How many pizzas did they have?

1109
01:14:00,919 --> 01:14:04,638
Nicolaides, yeah, he had two
in the shop. The other two had one each.

1110
01:14:04,639 --> 01:14:09,078
Balfe tossed his box in my face
when I asked him for the money.

1111
01:14:09,079 --> 01:14:11,838
I remember he was wearing,
like, black gloves.

1112
01:14:11,839 --> 01:14:13,758
No thumbs. All The Trigger wear 'em.

1113
01:14:13,759 --> 01:14:15,558
You're a liar. You liar.

1114
01:14:15,559 --> 01:14:18,838
Dock officer. Take Balfe to a cell
and leave him there.

1115
01:14:18,839 --> 01:14:20,469
You as well, dog.

1116
01:14:26,199 --> 01:14:29,589
Is your pizza parlour profitable?

1117
01:14:29,679 --> 01:14:33,278
Some weeks are good, some not.
You don't do it to lose money.

1118
01:14:33,279 --> 01:14:36,461
To that end, you save what you can.

1119
01:14:36,519 --> 01:14:42,429
You sometimes recycle the boxes
customers leave behind, don't you?

1120
01:14:43,279 --> 01:14:46,279
You do recycle some of the boxes?

1121
01:14:46,999 --> 01:14:49,838
That's good.
It helps reduce the litter mountain.

1122
01:14:49,839 --> 01:14:52,112
Only if the box is clean.

1123
01:14:52,439 --> 01:14:57,318
Well, did the defendants finish
their pizzas in your takeaway?

1124
01:14:57,319 --> 01:15:00,718
- I don't remember.
- I think you do, Mr Post.

1125
01:15:00,719 --> 01:15:02,998
And you reused their boxes.

1126
01:15:02,999 --> 01:15:06,318
That was how Mr Balfe's print
was on the box,

1127
01:15:06,319 --> 01:15:08,718
that was subsequently taken by someone

1128
01:15:08,719 --> 01:15:11,118
to the hotel room. Wasn't it?

1129
01:15:11,119 --> 01:15:14,798
My lord, this witness could not possibly
know the answer to this.

1130
01:15:14,799 --> 01:15:16,709
Yes, I tend to agree.

1131
01:15:17,199 --> 01:15:22,381
Unless Mr Mason Allen is
suggesting Mr Post took it there.

1132
01:15:24,679 --> 01:15:28,118
My lord, that was our last witness
and concludes the case for the Crown.

1133
01:15:28,119 --> 01:15:34,301
Thank you, Mr Sorrell. Am I going
to hear from anyone on the defence?

1134
01:15:39,879 --> 01:15:43,607
No, my lord.
I'd like to call Jesus Balfe.

1135
01:15:43,879 --> 01:15:47,358
I assume you will allow him back
to give evidence.

1136
01:15:47,359 --> 01:15:50,318
He's had enough to say from the dock,
Mr Mason Allen.

1137
01:15:50,319 --> 01:15:54,518
I'm fascinated to hear what
he's going to say from the witness box.

1138
01:15:54,519 --> 01:15:56,718
It weren't me that shot him.

1139
01:15:56,719 --> 01:15:59,478
I wasn't at the hotel
like that slam said.

1140
01:15:59,479 --> 01:16:01,934
I didn't even know Jackson.

1141
01:16:01,999 --> 01:16:07,272
And where were you
at the approximate time of the shooting?

1142
01:16:09,199 --> 01:16:13,558
We was at that dog's pizza takeaway.
We didn't take nothing away.

1143
01:16:13,559 --> 01:16:15,278
We ate our pizzas there.

1144
01:16:15,279 --> 01:16:18,398
I tossed my box back
when he said we shouldn't eat there.

1145
01:16:18,399 --> 01:16:20,758
We was there all that time.

1146
01:16:20,759 --> 01:16:23,518
Then we was at Dave's house,
playing some tunes.

1147
01:16:23,519 --> 01:16:26,156
Ask his gran. Go on, ask her.

1148
01:16:27,919 --> 01:16:32,438
Mr Balfe, you say you didn't know
the dead man, Mr Jackson.

1149
01:16:32,439 --> 01:16:34,899
Yeah, never heard of him.

1150
01:16:34,999 --> 01:16:37,158
Well, yet, like you, he dealt in drugs,

1151
01:16:37,159 --> 01:16:40,038
and was trying to move on to ground
controlled by The Trigger.

1152
01:16:40,039 --> 01:16:41,758
We don't deal with drugs.

1153
01:16:41,759 --> 01:16:44,598
I never heard of that man
till the police come for me.

1154
01:16:44,599 --> 01:16:48,278
Are you quite sure you didn't know
Mr Jackson before he was shot dead?

1155
01:16:48,279 --> 01:16:50,638
I told you, I never heard of him.

1156
01:16:50,639 --> 01:16:53,118
Yet, three days before he was shot,

1157
01:16:53,119 --> 01:16:57,918
you were caught on a CCTV camera
in Harlesden Parade arguing with him.

1158
01:16:57,919 --> 01:16:59,878
Perhaps if we take a look
at that footage again,

1159
01:16:59,879 --> 01:17:03,438
you might tell us if it's you. Mr Usher.

1160
01:17:03,439 --> 01:17:05,258
Exhibit 12, my lord.

1161
01:17:28,799 --> 01:17:30,358
Is that you, Mr Balfe?

1162
01:17:30,359 --> 01:17:34,198
Sure. Any fool can see that.
The other dude, I don't know.

1163
01:17:34,199 --> 01:17:36,158
He was just some bitch who dissed me,

1164
01:17:36,159 --> 01:17:40,118
so I set him straight,
like I do when people diss me.

1165
01:17:40,119 --> 01:17:42,958
I didn't see him before, or after.

1166
01:17:42,959 --> 01:17:46,718
- And what were you arguing about?
- I don't know. How should I know?

1167
01:17:46,719 --> 01:17:49,558
A man you argued with
is shot dead three days later,

1168
01:17:49,559 --> 01:17:51,358
and you expect the jury to believe

1169
01:17:51,359 --> 01:17:53,918
that you don't remember
what you argued about?

1170
01:17:53,919 --> 01:17:56,878
I said I don't.
He was just some punk who dissed me.

1171
01:17:56,879 --> 01:17:58,509
So you shot him?

1172
01:18:02,319 --> 01:18:04,410
I remember what he did.

1173
01:18:05,599 --> 01:18:09,038
He looked at me in a way I didn't like
coming out of the shop.

1174
01:18:09,039 --> 01:18:11,312
So I had a word with him.

1175
01:18:12,079 --> 01:18:16,518
- He had a lot of mouth and no respect.
- So you killed him?

1176
01:18:16,519 --> 01:18:18,158
I've already told you. You deaf?

1177
01:18:18,159 --> 01:18:21,250
I'm sure the jury heard, Mr Balfe.

1178
01:18:41,479 --> 01:18:44,798
My apologies for being late, my lord.

1179
01:18:44,799 --> 01:18:49,438
I was just about to invite Mr Sorrell
to give your closing argument, Mr Mills.

1180
01:18:49,439 --> 01:18:53,076
- Are you ready with it?
- I am, my lord.

1181
01:18:56,319 --> 01:18:58,838
Ladies and gentlemen of the jury,

1182
01:18:58,839 --> 01:19:00,998
a man was shot dead in a hotel room

1183
01:19:00,999 --> 01:19:04,678
following an earlier dispute
over drug turf.

1184
01:19:04,679 --> 01:19:05,918
Not the everyday occurrence

1185
01:19:05,919 --> 01:19:10,438
that affects the lives of ordinary,
decent people, you might think.

1186
01:19:10,439 --> 01:19:13,198
Not a death that we should have
much concern about,

1187
01:19:13,199 --> 01:19:15,518
any more than we should
be concerned about the life

1188
01:19:15,519 --> 01:19:21,429
of someone who deals in drugs,
save for all the misery they cause.

1189
01:19:21,959 --> 01:19:24,419
But if we think like that,

1190
01:19:24,519 --> 01:19:28,338
then as a society,
we start to become lost.

1191
01:19:29,279 --> 01:19:32,838
It means we think that these people
who deal in drugs

1192
01:19:32,839 --> 01:19:37,930
and settle their differences with guns
are above the law.

1193
01:19:38,159 --> 01:19:39,918
No one is above the law.

1194
01:19:39,919 --> 01:19:43,238
Not if we are to have
a flourishing, cohesive,

1195
01:19:43,239 --> 01:19:46,149
and most of all, a fair society.

1196
01:19:48,199 --> 01:19:51,789
Reasonable doubt, ladies and gentlemen.

1197
01:19:52,879 --> 01:19:54,478
That's all you need to have

1198
01:19:54,479 --> 01:19:58,918
in order to acquit Jez Balfe
and his co-defendants of this murder.

1199
01:19:58,919 --> 01:20:01,149
Reasonable doubt.

1200
01:20:02,399 --> 01:20:04,036
And there is much,

1201
01:20:04,039 --> 01:20:06,438
both in the evidence that was presented
here in court,

1202
01:20:06,439 --> 01:20:09,269
and in the absence of evidence,

1203
01:20:09,359 --> 01:20:13,198
to cause you to doubt
that these three young men

1204
01:20:13,199 --> 01:20:15,549
killed Simon Jackson.

1205
01:20:18,479 --> 01:20:20,629
No indisputable motive,

1206
01:20:21,879 --> 01:20:25,109
no clear, undeniable opportunity,

1207
01:20:26,359 --> 01:20:28,998
no presence
of the defendant's fingerprints

1208
01:20:28,999 --> 01:20:32,909
clinging to a murder weapon
to condemn them.

1209
01:20:35,279 --> 01:20:37,069
Reasonable doubt?

1210
01:20:38,159 --> 01:20:39,709
Indeed.

1211
01:20:58,639 --> 01:21:00,398
Which way you gonna vote, Paul?

1212
01:21:00,399 --> 01:21:03,469
What? I can't talk about that.

1213
01:21:04,159 --> 01:21:06,523
The judge said we mustn't.

1214
01:21:10,519 --> 01:21:17,065
But you know as well as we do that Jez
and his mates didn't pop that guy.

1215
01:21:17,319 --> 01:21:20,047
We know where you live, blood.

1216
01:21:23,119 --> 01:21:25,392
And where your mum lives.

1217
01:21:27,559 --> 01:21:29,069
You all right?

1218
01:21:50,999 --> 01:21:53,636
The jury's coming back. Come.

1219
01:21:57,159 --> 01:21:59,796
Will the foreman please rise?

1220
01:22:01,839 --> 01:22:05,718
Have you reached a verdict
in relation to the three defendants?

1221
01:22:05,719 --> 01:22:06,749
We have.

1222
01:22:06,839 --> 01:22:10,238
- Is that a unanimous verdict?
- Yes, it is.

1223
01:22:10,239 --> 01:22:13,589
In relation to Jesus Balfe,

1224
01:22:13,679 --> 01:22:16,998
on the charge of murder,
how do you find him?

1225
01:22:16,999 --> 01:22:18,909
Guilty or not guilty?

1226
01:22:26,439 --> 01:22:27,869
Guilty.

1227
01:22:34,399 --> 01:22:37,309
- Clear the court.
- You're dead.

1228
01:22:40,079 --> 01:22:41,669
You're dead.

1229
01:22:46,719 --> 01:22:48,199
You're dead.

1230
01:22:57,599 --> 01:23:01,145
Order has been restored, Judge.

1231
01:23:01,559 --> 01:23:03,318
They're ready
with the other two verdicts.

1232
01:23:03,319 --> 01:23:05,029
Good.

1233
01:23:05,119 --> 01:23:09,029
Judge, the jury foreman told the bailiff

1234
01:23:09,119 --> 01:23:11,798
he was threatened
by one of the defendants' friends.

1235
01:23:11,799 --> 01:23:15,598
- How did he get to him?
- Dressed as a security guard.

1236
01:23:15,599 --> 01:23:19,358
The police think he's the one
that drove the security van

1237
01:23:19,359 --> 01:23:21,359
that killed Reb Spark.

1238
01:23:24,039 --> 01:23:27,403
He's locked in court with the others.

1239
01:23:28,759 --> 01:23:30,029
Good.

1240
01:23:31,799 --> 01:23:33,878
I'll deal with the verdicts first.

1241
01:23:33,879 --> 01:23:35,970
On the charge of murder

1242
01:23:35,999 --> 01:23:40,109
in relation to David Nicolaides,

1243
01:23:40,199 --> 01:23:43,927
how do you find him?
Guilty or not guilty?

1244
01:23:44,519 --> 01:23:45,839
Guilty.

1245
01:23:45,919 --> 01:23:50,374
On the charge of murder
in relation to Sky Rudkin,

1246
01:23:50,439 --> 01:23:53,998
how do you find him?
Guilty or not guilty?

1247
01:23:53,999 --> 01:23:55,399
Not guilty.

1248
01:23:56,679 --> 01:24:01,589
In the alternative to murder,
in respect of Sky Rudkin,

1249
01:24:01,639 --> 01:24:06,367
do you find him guilty or not guilty
of manslaughter?

1250
01:24:06,679 --> 01:24:07,999
Guilty.

1251
01:24:11,039 --> 01:24:14,130
Thank you, you can sit down.

1252
01:24:15,279 --> 01:24:20,318
Members of the jury,
I offer you the court's sincere thanks

1253
01:24:20,319 --> 01:24:22,118
for your courage and determination

1254
01:24:22,119 --> 01:24:25,358
to see this trial through
to its conclusion.

1255
01:24:25,359 --> 01:24:30,478
I further discharge you from ever
having to do jury service again.

1256
01:24:30,479 --> 01:24:34,278
Perhaps you would remain seated
while I deal with sentencing.

1257
01:24:34,279 --> 01:24:35,909
Thank you.

1258
01:24:38,639 --> 01:24:42,518
Would those of you on the public seats
over there please stand?

1259
01:24:42,519 --> 01:24:43,839
Come on.

1260
01:24:43,919 --> 01:24:48,919
And state your name and relationship
with the prisoners.

1261
01:24:49,519 --> 01:24:51,798
Doreen Balfe. I'm Jez's mum.

1262
01:24:51,799 --> 01:24:53,509
James Hay, friend.

1263
01:24:53,599 --> 01:24:55,598
Mono Simons, cousin to Jez.

1264
01:24:55,599 --> 01:24:57,878
- Bea waxman.
- Natalie wallis.

1265
01:24:57,879 --> 01:24:59,879
Andy Thompson, friend.

1266
01:25:01,159 --> 01:25:03,619
Del Peabody, friend.

1267
01:25:03,879 --> 01:25:05,798
He's the one.

1268
01:25:05,799 --> 01:25:07,149
Thank you.

1269
01:25:07,439 --> 01:25:12,038
Would a police officer take Mr Peabody
to a cell, please, pending charges?

1270
01:25:12,039 --> 01:25:14,198
- For what?
- You'll find out.

1271
01:25:14,199 --> 01:25:18,381
What's wrong with you, man?
For what? What now?

1272
01:25:18,879 --> 01:25:20,698
Get off me.

1273
01:25:25,919 --> 01:25:29,118
I have been watching those
on the public seats very carefully.

1274
01:25:29,119 --> 01:25:31,838
Some of you, I've allowed to leave.

1275
01:25:31,839 --> 01:25:33,509
Those that remain,

1276
01:25:33,959 --> 01:25:36,959
your behaviour has been shameful.

1277
01:25:37,879 --> 01:25:41,478
As a result,
I'm charging you all with contempt.

1278
01:25:41,479 --> 01:25:44,038
You will remain in custody
until you've purged that contempt.

1279
01:25:44,039 --> 01:25:48,078
That means that you will make a humble
and sincere apology to this court,

1280
01:25:48,079 --> 01:25:50,625
and especially to this jury,

1281
01:25:50,799 --> 01:25:56,038
whom you have assaulted with your
spitting and threatening behaviour.

1282
01:25:56,039 --> 01:25:58,838
Now, you will make that apology
right now from where you are,

1283
01:25:58,839 --> 01:26:03,158
and make it you will,
or find yourselves locked up.

1284
01:26:03,159 --> 01:26:04,269
Yeah.

1285
01:26:05,399 --> 01:26:07,709
I apologise, sir.

1286
01:26:08,199 --> 01:26:11,109
I'm very sorry for my behaviour.

1287
01:26:11,839 --> 01:26:13,709
Good. You may sit.

1288
01:26:17,599 --> 01:26:20,690
Is no one else going to apologise?

1289
01:26:24,159 --> 01:26:25,669
No one.

1290
01:26:25,759 --> 01:26:29,078
All right, you'll be held for contempt
until dealt with by another judge

1291
01:26:29,079 --> 01:26:31,438
in due course.
Will the police officers take them out?

1292
01:26:31,439 --> 01:26:34,278
You can't do that,
they've got families to look after.

1293
01:26:34,279 --> 01:26:37,118
- What's your name, madam?
- Mrs Coffee, Dave's grandmother.

1294
01:26:37,119 --> 01:26:39,118
Well, Mrs Coffee,
unless you want to join them,

1295
01:26:39,119 --> 01:26:41,158
you will apologise to the court as well.

1296
01:26:41,159 --> 01:26:44,238
Like hell I will. You'll rot in hell
for what you've just done.

1297
01:26:44,239 --> 01:26:47,238
I may well do that, but in
the meanwhile, you will go to prison.

1298
01:26:47,239 --> 01:26:51,118
- Take her out.
- Get off me. I hope you get cancer.

1299
01:26:51,119 --> 01:26:52,469
Get off me.

1300
01:26:54,919 --> 01:26:58,198
I can't be here.
I've got to go to the hospital.

1301
01:26:58,199 --> 01:27:02,158
Look what he done. Put him in jail.
He assaulted me, you saw that.

1302
01:27:02,159 --> 01:27:04,118
I saw now such thing, Mr Balfe.

1303
01:27:04,119 --> 01:27:08,278
All I saw was you attacking a juror
who righteously defended himself.

1304
01:27:08,279 --> 01:27:11,638
I'm sure the prison medical authorities
can easily deal with that,

1305
01:27:11,639 --> 01:27:12,958
because that's where you're going.

1306
01:27:12,959 --> 01:27:16,638
First of all, I would like an apology
from each of you for your contempt.

1307
01:27:16,639 --> 01:27:20,458
- What's that, a joke?
- He's a joker, man.

1308
01:27:20,719 --> 01:27:23,083
I'm very sorry, sir. I am.

1309
01:27:23,479 --> 01:27:24,918
Thank you, Mr Rudkin.

1310
01:27:24,919 --> 01:27:27,558
I'm going to want
further reports on Rudkin.

1311
01:27:27,559 --> 01:27:29,358
Can we have him back for sentencing
in three weeks?

1312
01:27:29,359 --> 01:27:32,909
- Yes, my lord. The 29th?
- 29th, yeah.

1313
01:27:45,799 --> 01:27:50,478
It gives me no pleasure
in giving you each a life sentence,

1314
01:27:50,479 --> 01:27:53,116
as I am bound to, for murder.

1315
01:27:53,239 --> 01:27:58,149
That means that you will each serve
30 years in prison.

1316
01:27:58,159 --> 01:27:59,669
Take them down.

1317
01:28:13,959 --> 01:28:15,069
Jo.

1318
01:28:16,479 --> 01:28:20,958
- You collecting Michael?
- Yeah, I don't want to be late.

1319
01:28:20,959 --> 01:28:24,398
- Can I give you a lift? I'd like to.
- Are you sure, John?

1320
01:28:24,399 --> 01:28:28,945
He badly needs people in his life
who are constant.

